1 NOTE {geni:about_me}  
2 CONT
2 CONT #  Benjamin ROSENBERGER
2 CONT
2 CONT # Surname: Rosenberger
2 CONT
2 CONT # Given Name: Benjamin
2 CONT
2 CONT # Sex: M
2 CONT
2 CONT # Birth: 1715 in Zweibrucken, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT # Death: 1777 in Hatfield Twp., Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT # _UID: 2CBB904F39F305439595B875D17A5C215921
2 CONT
2 CONT # Change Date: 26 Feb 2006 at 22:17:57
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Heinrich ROSENBERGER Sr. b: ABT 1690 in Zweibrucken, Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Barbara b: 1692 in Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Helena DELP b: ABT 1720 in Germany
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Elias (Yellis) ROSENBERGER b: 1734/1735 (Abt 1742?) in Hatfield Twp., Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. John ROSENBERGER b: abt 1736/1737 (Abt 1744) in Hatfield Twp., Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT    3. Gertrude ROSENBERGER b: ABT 1746 in Hatfield Twp., Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT    4. Elizabeth ROSENBERGER b: 9 Feb 1748 in Hatfield Twp., Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT    5. Henry ROSENBERGER b: ABT 1749/1750 in Montgomery Co., PA
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT  Benjamin Rosenberger was born in 1718 at Zweibrucken, DE. He married Helena Delp before 1735. Benjamin Rosenberger died in 1777 at Hatfield, Montgomery, PA, US.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Helena Delp b. c 1720, d. 1799
2 CONT
2 CONT Child	
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Johannes John D. Rosenberger+ b. 1737, d. 1834 __________________________________________________ [The Rosenbergers are descended from Henry Rosenberger, Wurtemburg,  Germany, and settled in Franconia township, Montgomery co
2 CONC unty, prior to  1729. He bought 159 acres of James Steel, Philadelphia, and Michael  Swartley, of the fifth generation, now occupies it. He was buried at  Franconia Mennonite Church. His children were Benjamin, Daniel, John and  H
2 CONC enry, the two eldest supposed to have been born in Germany. In 1739  Benjamin Rosenberger bought 125 acres on the county line, including the O.  G. Morris farm, died in 1777, and his widow, Helen, 1799. His son Elias,  who lived a
2 CONC nd died in Rockhill, owned land in Springfield, Bedminster and  Hilltown, where he lived at various periods of his life. He had four  children, Benjamin, driving a baggage wagon in the Continental army before  reaching manhood, sp
2 CONC ending the latter part of his life in Rockhill. John,  son of Benjamin, Sr., owned a farm in Hatfield, Montgomery county, whose  sons Benjamin and Jacob, and daughters, Anna and Susannah removed to  Canada. Both daughters became t
2 CONC he wives of the Rev. Jacob Detweiler. A  granddaughter of Benjamin, a daughter of John Alderfer, became the wife of  Bishop Abraham Hunsicker, who founded Freeland Seminary, the parent of  Ursinus College. Henry, another son of Be
2 CONC njamin Rosenberger, and his  children are much scattered. Daniel, the second son of Henry Rosenberger,  Sr., in 1740, bought 359 acres in Hatfield township, on the county line,  where he lived and died. His will mentions children,
2 CONC  David, who married  Ann Funk, Isaac married Christiana Funk, Ann married Michael Kolb, and  Mary married Valentine Kratz. John Rosenberger, third son of Henry, Sr.,  born 1724, and died 1808, was buried at Fricks graveyard. He ow
2 CONC ned several  hundred acres including the site of Hatfield village and left a farm to  each child. He erected the first grist-mill in that section which stood  until 1820, and was one of the founders of Line Lexington Mennonite  ch
2 CONC urch. His children were Martin, Abraham, John, Benjamin, Daniel, Henry  and Catharine, wife of Abraham Allebach. Many of the descendants live in  Bucks county. The fourth son of Henry Rosenberger, Sr., was the Rev. Henry  Rosenber
2 CONC ger, born December 2, 1752, married Barbara Oberholtzer, 1745, and  settled in New Britain on the Hilltown line near Lexington. He was a M ennonite minister and served the Franconia church. His father gave him the  homestead short
2 CONC ly after his marriage, and he spent his life there. His  children were:
2 CONT      Gertrude 
2 CONT     Annie, born 1748, married Michael Leatherman, second husband John Loux, 
2 CONT        grandfather of the late John A. Loux
2 CONT     Abraham 
2 CONT     Elizabeth, born 1752, married Mark Fretz, New Britain
2 CONT     Barbara, born 1755, married David Rickert and died at 20
2 CONT     Magdalina married John Swartley and 
2 CONT     Sarah married Philip Swartley.*]
2 CONT
2 CONT     [A reunion of the Rosenberger family was held in Perkasie Park, August  10, 1899, to which 1,500 descendants of the pioneer were invited and it is  thought as many attended. The exercises consisted of an address of  welcome, b
2 CONC y Prof. Rosenberger, Quakertown, vocal and instrumental music,  historical address by Rev. A. J. Fretz, Milton, N.J., other addresses, and  dinner, not the least attractive part of the programme.*]    
2 CONT
2 CONT http://files.usgwarchives.net/pa/bucks/history/local/davis/davis33.txt
2 CONT
