1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Barbara Moyer Cassel=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Reverend Yelles (Julius) Cassel and Susanna "Anna" Cassel (Meyer)
2 CONT * Birth:	October 25, 1731 in Franconia, PA, British Colonial America 
2 CONT * Death:	December 29, 1792 (61) in Franconia, Montgomery, Pennsylvania, British Colonial America
2 CONT * Place of Burial: Delps Cemetery, Harleysville, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania,  British Colonial America.
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/37503343/barbara-moyer-funckin '''Barbara Moyer Cassel Find A Grave Memorial''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married: [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000007835350745?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000175764617045 '''Rev. Christian Meyer Funk,Sr.,'''] son of Bishop Heinrich Funck, II and Anna Christina Meyer. Barbara Cassel, 
2 CONC m. Christian Funk; he was born 1731 and married 1757, and was called to the ministry about the same time; he died May 31, 1811, in his 80th year, and is buried in Delp's graveyard, in Franconia township, Montgomery county, Pa.; hi
2 CONC s wife Barbara died December 29, 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000216441543836&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Anna Cassel Funk Rosenberger,''' 1755–1790
2 CONT * '''Elizabeth Funk Gotwals,''' 1756–1817
2 CONT * '''Mary Funk,''' born in 1757. She was married in Skippack in 1784. There is no other record of her life or of her husband.
2 CONT * '''Catherine Funk Detweiler,''' 1759–1808
2 CONT * '''Barbara Funk Reiff,''' 1760–1798
2 CONT * '''Sarah Funk,''' born in 1761. She married Michael Brodt in 1787.
2 CONT * '''John Funk,''' born in 1762. He married Ann Johnson in 1788 in Skippack. Ann was born in Skippack in 1761. John was a Funkite minister and a farmer. In 1811 he inherited his father's farm. During the War of 1812 John attempted
2 CONC  speculating (investing in real estate, stocks and bonds.) but could not make his payments to his father's estate as a result of his investments. His son bought the farm and John bought another farm in Berks County.  John and Ann 
2 CONC had 5 children. Ann (Johnson) Funk died May 25, 1797 in Skippack and was buried in Lower Skippack Mennonite Cemetery. John married Elizabeth Wagner in 1798. They had 4 children. John Funk died in Amnity Township, Berks County, PA.
2 CONT * '''Abraham Funk,''' 1764–1796
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000216468566821&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000216468566822&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Rev. Christian Meyer Funk, Sr, by North America, Family Histories, Cassel A genealogical history of the Cassel family in America : being the descendants of Julius Kassel.=
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000007835350745?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000175765633043 '''Page 259''']
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000007835350745?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000175764676936 '''Page 260''']
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000007835350745?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000175764649966 '''Page 261''']
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000007835350745&photo_id=6000000175775734831&position=0 '''Page 262''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://archive.org/details/genealogicalhist00cass/page/256/mode/2up?q=Barbara+ A genealogical history of the Cassel family in America : being the descendants of Julius Kassel or Yelles Cassel, of Kriesheim, Baden, Germany : co
2 CONC ntaining biographical sketches of prominent descendants, with illustrations, by Cassel, Daniel Kolb]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Page 256'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon after his marriage he bought a farm of one hundred and six acres of land in Hilltown, Bucks county, Pa. It appears he was a man of limited means. The greater portion of the purchase money was advanced and secured on first mor
2 CONC tgage, on the delivery of the deed to Hupert Cassel, by Rev. Christian Funk, of Franconia township ; the mortgage, however, was satisfied in the year 1763. Hupert Cassel was a brother to Barbara, wife of Rev. Christian Funk. Again
2 CONC , in the year 1775, this same Hupert Cassel bought another tract adjoining the aforesaid one hundred and six acres, containing about seventy-two acres, of Philip Fluck and Christian Kern.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=  
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian Meyer Funk was born in Franconia, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania. He was a son of Henry (1702-1760) and Anna (Meyer) Funk (1707-1758) who were the parents of 10 children.  Barbara Moyer Cassel was born in Franconia in 1
2 CONC 731. She was a daughter of Yelles (1679-1750) and Anna (Meyer) Cassel (1688-1755) who had 9 children. Christian and Barbara were married in 1751 in Franconia. Christian and Barbara lived at Christian's parents' homestead at Indian
2 CONC  Creek . After his father's death in 1760 Christian inherited 137 acres 143 perches of the land with the house built by his father and his brother Abraham inherited 63 acres 19 perches  with the mill and other improvements built b
2 CONC y his father. In 1763 Christian purchased the remainder of his father's homestead and mill from Abraham. Abraham began serving as a minister in the Franconia Mennonite Conference after being ordained by his father in 1756 and he b
2 CONC ecame minister of Franconia Mennonite church in 1757. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As support grew for rebellion against British rule and taxes were imposed to finance the Revolutionary War the members of the Mennonite church developed a rift over whether they should or should not support the war effort. Christi
2 CONC an did not oppose the war. , in fact he favored Colonial independence and willingly paid the war tax. He was eventually expelled from the Franconia Mennonite Conference after which he and the other like minded Mennonites formed a 
2 CONC sect of the church called Funkites, a group which disintegrated in 1855. Although Christian supported the war he refused to report for military duty after being drafted. Fines were levied against him for his refusal. He appealed t
2 CONC he fines on the ground that he was a minister at the time of the draft but his appeal was denied. Christian made a living as a miller, farmer, and minister. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''He and Barbara had 9 children:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Anna Funk''' was a GG Great Grandmother of Vera Shelly (Hummel) Garis: Anna (Funk)/ David Rosenberger > David Rosenberger/ Catharine (Delp) > Barbara (Rosenberger)/ Christian B. Shelly > John R. Shelly/ Susanna (Diehl) > K
2 CONC atharine D. (Shelly)/ Harry Franklin B. Hummel > Vera Shelly (Hummel) Garis
2 CONT * 2. '''Elizabeth Funk''' was born in 1755 . She married Henry Gotwals in 1776 in Franconia. Henry was born in 1749. He served during the Revolutionary War as a PVT 3rd Class in Captain Weaver's Company of Philadelphia County Mili
2 CONC tia's 1st battalion. Elizabeth and Henry had 5 sons and Henry made a living as a farmer. Elizabeth (Funk) Gotwals died March 26, 1817 and Henry Gotwals died July 25, 1832. They were buried at the Funkite (Providence Mennonite) Cem
2 CONC etery, Evansburg, Montgomery County, PA.
2 CONT * 3. '''Mary Funk''' was born in 1757. She was married in Skippack in 1784. There is no other record of her life or of her husband.
2 CONT * 4. '''Catherine Funk''' was a GG Great  Grandmother of Elmer D. Garis: Catherine (Funk)/John K. Detweiler. > John F. Detweiler/ Anna H. (Detweiler) > Anna D. (Detweiler)/ John F. Detweiler > Catharine D. (Detweiler)/ Joseph S, G
2 CONC aris > Frank D. Garis/ Ida S. (Detweiler) > Elmer D. Garis
2 CONT * 5.'''Barbara Funk''' was born June 22, 1760. She married John Reiff in 1780  in Skippack. John was born in 1759.   in Towamencin which was later bequeathed to the German Baptist church through the will of John's and Barbara's so
2 CONC n John F. Reiff. on August 14. 1830. Barbara and John had 5 children. Barbara (Funk) Reiff died July 27, 1798 in Lower Salford, Montgomery County, PA. John Reiff died February 6, 1786. They were buried in Lower Dkippack Mennonite 
2 CONC Cemetery, Skippack, Montgomery County, PA.
2 CONT * 6. '''Sarah Funk''' was born in 1761. She married Michael Brodt in 1787.
2 CONT * 7. '''John Funk''' was born in 1762. He married Ann Johnson in 1788 in Skippack. Ann was born in Skippack in 1761. John was a Funkite minister and a farmer. In 1811 he inherited his father's farm. During the War of 1812 John att
2 CONC empted speculating (investing in real estate, stocks and bonds.) but could not make his payments to his father's estate as a result of his investments. His son bought the farm and John bought another farm in Berks County.  John an
2 CONC d Ann had 5 children. Ann (Johnson) Funk died May 25, 1797 in Skippack and was buried in Lower Skippack Mennonite Cemetery. John married Elizabeth Wagner in 1798. They had 4 children. John Funk died in Amnity Township, Berks Count
2 CONC y, PA.
2 CONT * 8. '''Abraham Funk''' was born in 1764. He married and had at least 1 child. He died July 13, 1796 and was buried in Towamencin Mennonite Churchyard, Kulpsville, Montgomery County, PA. Susannah Funk was born in 1768. She married
2 CONC  in Montgomery County, PA in 1792. Her husband was born in 1765 and his last name was Detweiler.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1786 Christian and Barbara moved to Virginia where Christian served as a minister in the Virginia Mennonite Conference. After some time he returned to his home in Pennsylvania. In 1794 he sold 241 acres 51 perches of land with 
2 CONC a mill and other improvements to his son in law  John Detweiler. Barbara Moyer (Cassel) Funk died December 29, 1792, Christian Meyer Funk died July 31, 1811 in Harleysville, Montgomery County, Pa. They were buried in Delp's Cemete
2 CONC ry, Harleysville, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
