2 CONT =History=  
2 CONT
2 CONT Page information based upon 1) original research of Daniel W. Bly and published in his From The Rhine to The Shanendoah, Gateway Press, Inc., Baltimore, MD 21202, vol I. (1993), vol II. (1996)*; research of 2) Karen Kellogg Stephe
2 CONC n of Mehoopany,PA., 3) Christine Stephens of Ft. Thomas,KY and 4) Mike Judd.
2 CONT The origins of the Funk family have been well researched and articulated by Mr. Bly. I borrow shamelessly from him; please be aware that any and all errors be they ommission or commission are my own responsibility. This lineage is
2 CONC  believed to have been involved in the early Anabaptist movement in late 16th century Switzerland. Specifically Jacob Funk and his wife Anna Naff were born in Ober Mettmenstetten in the Knonau District of Canton Zurich about1575. 
2 CONC Later generations moved to Germany and the region known as the Palatinate where they are well documented in Mennonite historical documents. Their movement can be seen as a result both of their persecution by authorities for their 
2 CONC religious beliefs as well as their religious outreach activities. Heinrich Funk (abt 1662) was the first immigrant to the present USA in 1717 having left Bonfeld in Baden and settled with other family members in Strasburg Township
2 CONC , Pennsylvania. Later generations settled in Virginia and then Washinton Co, Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Immigration''': List 54 B] Palatines imported in the Snow Two Sisters, James Marshall, Commander, from Rotterdam, but last from Cowes in England. Qualified the 9th Day of September 1738. Heinrich Funk, age 36; Anna Funk age 31
2 CONT
2 CONT 1744 Wrote "Ein Spiegle Der Tauffe" mirror of baptism
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Notes for Anna Meyer:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Information on her ancestors is mostly from Jay D Weaver who is quoting information from "Martin Kendig's Swiss Relatives" by Jane Evans Best in the Pennsylvania Mennonite Heritage Publication, January, 1992. This is published by 
2 CONC the Lancaster Mennonite Historical Society, 2215 Millstream Road, Lancaster, PA 17602.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =History & Documents=  
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000006581100178&photo_id=6000000203709557823&position=0 '''The Original Deed and Wax Seal for Henry Funk's House. Picture taken at Mennonite Heritage Center by Gene
2 CONC  Funk and Posted to https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/17426869/henry-funk on January 4, 2013''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://archive.org/stream/briefhistoryofbi01fret#page/17/mode/1up/search/Christian+Funk '''The Last and Will Testiment of Henry Funck, pages 13-20''']
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_Funck Heinrich Funck, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Funck (c. 1697–1760) was a mill operator, religious author and a Mennonite bishop in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Funck is commonly believed to have been born in the Palatinate region of Germany. No baptismal record is known. He was a descendant of Swiss Mennonites who were expelled from Bern, Switzerland, in the 17th century based l
2 CONC argely on their religious beliefs. Funck arrived in Philadelphia in 1717 with his family and other German Palatines, seeking a place to freely practice their Mennonite faith, including Dielman Kolb (1691–1756), who became an early
2 CONC  Mennonite minister in Pennsylvania. During 1719, he settled in Franconia Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania. In about 1720, Funck married Anne Meyer (ca 1700–1758), daughter of Mennonite immigrant Christian Meyer (1676–175
2 CONC 1). They would be the parents of ten children.[3] Heinrich Funck was the grandfather of Joseph Funk and of Jacob Funk, the original owner of the historic Jacob Funk House and Barn.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1725, Heinrich Funck built and operated a grist mill on Indian Creek in Franconia Township in what is today Telford, Pennsylvania. In 1738, Heinrich Funck, together with others including Dielman Kolb and Christian Meyer, was in
2 CONC strumental in purchasing land for the Salford Mennonite meeting house. The Salford Mennonite Congregation itself had dated to 1717. He served for many years as bishop in the Franconia Mennonite Conference.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1745 to 1748, together with Dielman Kolb, he supervised the translation of the classic 1660 Dutch religious history Martyrs Mirror into the German language. Funck also wrote two German-language religious books: Ein Spiegel de
2 CONC r Taufe (A Mirror of Baptism), published in 1744, and Eine Restitution, Oder eine Erklaerung einiger Haupt-puncten des Gesetzes (Restitution, or an Explanation of Several Principal Points of the Law), published posthumously by his
2 CONC  children in 1763. Heinrich Funck died during 1760 and was buried in the Delp Burying Ground in Harleysville, Pennsylvania. A memorial gravestone there lists him as Henry Funk.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://books.google.com/books?id=CthMAAAAMAAJ&printsec=frontcover&source=gbs_ge_summary_r&cad=0#v=onepage&q&f=false A Brief History of Bishop Henry Funck and Other Funk Pioneers,By Abraham James Fretz]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham James Fretz, Mennonite Publishing Company, 1899 - 874 pages. A genealogy of the descendants of Henry Funck born in Europe. He immigrated to America in 1719 and settled at Indian Creek, Franconia Township, Montgomery County
2 CONC , Pennsylvania where he died in 1760. He married Anne Meyer.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://www.danielhaston.com/roots/richterswil/Zurich-Lancaster-Families.pdf '''Anabaptist Families from Canton Zurich to Lancaster County, 1633 to 1729: A Tour, by Jane Evans Best''']
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Kappel (4)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Adam Näf (NF13) and his Anabaptist wife, Kleiann Wäber (?WB5) lived at Kappel in 1588. In 1571 he and two of his brothers had been granted rights to the Sennhof (dairy farm) at Kappel because of the heroism of their father in the 
2 CONC battle of Kappel on October 11, 1531. Their grandfather was killed in the battle, as was Ulrich Zwingli, founder of the Reformed Church. The Cloister at Kappel (Fig. h) was begun in the twelfth century, and after the Reformation t
2 CONC he farms under its control were sold to the hereditary tenants, including the Näf family.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Hof Buch, Knonau (5)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT The cause of the last Anabaptist persecution occurred in 1635 when Heinrich Frick (FR316) from Hof Buch in the parish of Knonau (Fig. i) resigned his office of Fandrich (standard bearer or ensign), to join the Anabaptists. His ref
2 CONC usal to bear arms came less than two years after invasion of Zurich territory by the Swedish army, and officials feared others might follow his exam in Kunheim, Alsace, in 1660 when he signed the Dortrecht Confession. His cousin, 
2 CONC Jacob Frick (FR334), was a servant in his household from 1634 to 1640, and was an Anabaptist living in Mussig, Alsace, in 1684. He was probably the grandfather of the Jacob Frick (FR3341.1) who came to Lancaster County in 1733.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Mettmenstetten (6)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1634 Heinrich Funk (FH1) was married in Mettmenstetten nearby to Cathrina Meylin (ML26), daughter of the Anabaptist minister. They had fourteen children, and some of their descendants came to North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000198381306860 From Switzerland to Quincy, Illious History of our Funks]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich Funck (d. 1760), a European Mennonite settler who located along the Indian Creek in what is now Franconia Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania, about 30 miles (50 km) north of Philadelphia. He married Anne, daughter 
2 CONC of immigrant Christian Meyer and they became the parents of ten children, including Preacher Henry Funck and Bishop Christian Funk (1731-1811), the latter being the founder of the Funkites, who withdrew from the Franconia Conferen
2 CONC ce in 1778 because they favored recognizing the independence of the American Colonies before the end of the Revolutionary War. Funck was a farmer, miller, and bishop. He wrote two books, Ein Spiegel der Tauffe (Mirror of Baptism) 
2 CONC in 1744, and Eine Restitution, Oder eine Erklaerung einiger Haupt-puncten des Gesetzes (Restitution, or an Explanation of Several Principal Points of the Law), the latter being left as an unpublished manuscript at his death in 176
2 CONC 0; his children arranged for its publication in 1763. Funck collaborated in sponsoring the publication of the 1748 German Martyrs' Mirror, and he and Dielman Kolb read the proofs page by page as they came off the Ephrata presses. 
2 CONC Funck was the most important writer among the Mennonites in Colonial America. His grave is lost, but it is known that he was intimately associated with both the Salford and Franconia congregations of the Franconia Conference - htt
2 CONC ps://gameo.org/index.php?title=Funck,_Heinrich_(d._1760)
2 CONT
2 CONT * Henry FUNCK was born about 1690 in Palatinate, Germany. He died in 1760 in Franconia Twp, Montgomery, PA. He was buried in Franconia Twp, Montgomery, PA. He has reference number 152. BIRTH: Born Heinrich Funck in either Holland 
2 CONC or Palatinate Abt 1690 
2 CONT
2 CONT * IMMIGRATION: Arrived in Philadelphia by 1718, lived in Germantown and, in 1719 settled at Indian Creek, Franconia Twp, Montgomery Co. PA. Said to have arrived with best friend Dielman Kolb, who landed 10 Aug 1717.  Often confuse
2 CONC d with another Heinnrich Funk who arrived in 1717 and settled in Lancaster Co, PA. (See Francis Funk pp. 16-20)  
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Title page of Eine Restitution, 1763 - https://www.geni.com/photo/view/6000000006581100178?album_type=photos_of_me&photo_id=6000000103826678285&position=0
2 CONT
2 CONT * LAND: 20 Dec 1718 land warrant to Heinrich Funck of Germantown for 101 3/4 acres on a branch of Perkeawming Creek. Later buys adjoining tracts. Total 201 acres. Also 166 acres "of ye Mannor Lands on ye upper part of a branch ", 
2 CONC prob part of Perkasie Manor, Hilltown Twp, Bucks Co, PA willed to son Henry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * PROBATE: Will filed in Philadelphia in Will No. 274, S427 dated 13 June 1759. Translated in A.J. Fretz pp. 13-20 and Frances Funck pp. 41-47 
2 CONT
2 CONT * OCCUPATION: First Mennonite Bishop in America; religious author and, translator, operated grist mill on Indian Creek, Franconia Twp, PA 
2 CONT
2 CONT * BURIAL: Delps Cemetary. Franconia Twp. This was the orignal Funk burying ground.  Stone marker erected for Bishop Funk. Near Bucks Co line near Telford, PA 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1718. Bishop Heinrich Funck, born Abt. 1702 in Germany; died 1760 in Franconia, Twp., Montgomery Co., PA. He was the son of 3436. Heinrich Funck and 3437. Franey. He married 1719. Anna Meyer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1719. Anna Meyer, born Abt. 1706 in Saulford, Montgomery Co., PA; died July 08, 1758 in Indian Creek, PA. She was the daughter of 3438. Christian Meyer and 3439. Barbara Bergey. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Notes for Bishop Heinrich Funck:
2 CONT Notes 
2 CONT From Tom Kirkpatrick: 
2 CONT BIRTH: Born Heinrich Funck in either Holland or Palatinate Abt 1690 
2 CONT
2 CONT * IMMIGRATION: Arrived in Philadelphia by 1718, lived in Germantown and, in 1719 settled at Indian Creek, Franconia Twp, Montgomery Co. PA. Said to have arrived with best friend Dielman Kolb, who landed 10 Aug 1717. Often confused
2 CONC  with another Heinnrich Funk who arrived in 1717 and settled in Lancaster Co, PA. (See Francis Funk pp. 16-20) 
2 CONT
2 CONT * LAND: 20 Dec 1718 land warrant to Heinrich Funck of Germantown for 101 3/4 acres on a branch of Perkeawming Creek. Later buys adjoining tracts. Total 201 acres. Also 166 acres "of ye Mannor Lands on ye upper part of a branch of 
2 CONC Perkyoming", prob part of Perkasie Manor, Hilltown Twp, Bucks Co, PA willed to son Henry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * PROBATE: Will filed in Philadelphia in Will No. 274, S427 dated 13 June 1759. Translated in A.J. Fretz pp. 13-20 and Frances Funck pp. 41-47 
2 CONT
2 CONT * OCCUPATION: First Mennonite Bishop in America; religious author and, translator, operated grist mill on Indian Creek, Franconia Twp, PA 
2 CONT
2 CONT * BURIAL: Delps Cemetary. Franconia Twp. This was the orignal Funk burying ground. Stone marker erected for Bishop Funk. Near Bucks Co line near Telford, PA 
2 CONT
2 CONT * SOURCES: Rev A.J. Fretz, A Brief History of Bishop Henry Funck and Other Funck 
2 CONT Pioneers;1709-1899; 1709-1899 ; Elkhart, IN, Mennonite Publishing Co, 1899; Reprint, Higginson Book Co, Salem, MA -Frances Funk, Joseph Funk A Biography; Pub 1984, Frances unk, HC72, Box 557, Lake of the Woods, Locust Grove, VA 22
2 CONC 508 -Richard & Kathryn Hott, Hott Ancestors; 1710-1984; Adelphi, MD, Mr. & Mr Richard D. Hott, 10423 Oakhill Ct, Adelphi, MD 20783 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Page information based upon 1) original research of Daniel W. Bly and published in his From The Rhine to The Shanendoah, Gateway Press, Inc., Baltimore, MD 21202, vol I. (1993), vol II. (1996)*; research of 2) Karen Kellogg Step
2 CONC hen of Mehoopany,PA., 3) Christine Stephens of Ft. Thomas,KY and 4) Mike Judd. 
2 CONT The origins of the Funk family have been well researched and articulated by Mr. Bly. I borrow shamelessly from him; please be aware that any and all errors be they ommission or commission are my own responsibility. This lineage is
2 CONC  believed to have been involved in the early Anabaptist movement in late 16th century Switzerland. Specifically Jacob Funk and his wife Anna Naff were born in Ober Mettmenstetten in the Knonau District of Canton Zurich about1575. 
2 CONC Later generations moved to Germany and the region known as the Palatinate where they are well documented in Mennonite historical documents. Their movement can be seen as a result both of their persecution by authorities for their 
2 CONC religious beliefs as well as their religious outreach activities. Heinrich Funk (abt 1662) was the first immigrant to the present USA in 1717 having left Bonfeld in Baden and settled with other family members in Strasburg Township
2 CONC , Pennsylvania. Later generations settled in Virginia and then Washinton Co, Kentucky 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Immigration: List 54 B] Palatines imported in the Snow Two Sisters, 
2 CONT James Marshall, Commander, from Rotterdam, but last from 
2 CONT Cowes in England. Qualified the 9th Day of September 
2 CONT 1738. Heinrich Funk, age 36; Anna Funk age 31 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1744 Wrote "Ein Spiegle Der Tauffe" mirror of baptism 
2 CONT
