2 CONC e residents of what is now upper Montgomery County, and accordi ng to Dr. William J. Hinke this petition furnished a good census of the Pe rkiomen Valley. Many of the men who signed the petition were Mennonites in cluding Hans Det
2 CONC weiler who was the nineteenth of twenty-seven signatur es on the petition to Governor John Roberts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hans Detweiler is listed among the first settlers of Berks and Philadelph ia counties (now Montgomery County), naturalized between 1729 and 1730 (Se ction 11 of the Appendix, page 434, in Rupp's Index to the 30,000 Nam es of Immig
2 CONC rants in Pennsylvania, 1727,1776) together with 83 other inhabi tants of Philadelphia County, most of these probably being from the area n ow known as Montgomery County. Since all of the English immigrants were B ritish subjects, 
2 CONC only immigrants of European extraction, many of them Menn onites, were required to take the oath of allegiance or become naturalize d. Hans was a weaver by trade and records show he owned land and paid qui trent prior to 1734. The
2 CONC  annual audit book, "The Skippack Alms book ," of the Mennonite church was signed twice between 1750 and 1757.
2 CONT
