2 CONT It is believed that Mary is the middle name of Anna Detweiler since in Han 's Account Book it states..."This is what Mary Dottweiller hath received from her father at her marriage Day in goods and others the value of... 40 17 01" 
2 CONC and "now what his daughter Susanna hath received from her father at her marriage Day to the value of... 39 07 07." There is no mention of the name Anna; however, Anna was married first to Hans Klemmer who d ied in 1737. In 1738 sh
2 CONC e married Frederick Alderfer and was still alive w hen her father died. As Alderfers of America states "She was known as Anna to her contemporaries."
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth: 	1685, Switzerland Death: 	Jun., 1761 Skippack Montgomery County Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes (Hans) Detweiler arrived in America prior to 1724. He was living in Bebber's Twp. (later Skippack Twp.) when he purchased land on 8 Apr 1724. He died June, 1761. He is listed among the first settlers of Berks and Montg. C
2 CONC o. (originally Phila. Co.). He and wife, Susanna were most likely Mennonites by religion, and probably lived in Switzerland before coming to America. .
2 CONT Hans Detweiler (Detwiler, Dettweiler) probably came to PA between 1710 and 1720. Whether he was married at the time of immigration is not known; however, his wife's name was Susanna. On 8 April 1724 Matthias VanBebber and his wife
2 CONC  Hermana granted to Hans Tottweiler of Bebber's Twp., Phila. Co., for 25 pounds, 102 acres, 52 perches (Deed book 11, page 238, Montgomery Co. Court House). In 1725 Hans Tetweiler with thirty-four others, signed a petition which w
2 CONC as presented to the Court of Quarter Sessions of Philadelphia ordering the surveying and formation of the Township of Skippack and Perkiomen. At that time Skippack and the surrounding townships were part of Philadelphia Co., since
2 CONC  Montgomery Co. was not established until 1784. .
2 CONT Although Indians were not much of a problem for the pioneers of Bebber's Twp., due to the Indian situation farther west around Falkner's Swamp "The 1728 Petition for Protection agains the Indians" was signed by most of the residen
2 CONC ts of what is now Upper Montg. Co., and according to Dr. William J. Hinke this petition furnished a good census of the Perkiomen Valley. Many of the men who signed this petition were Mennonites including Hans Detweiler who was the
2 CONC  nineteenth of seventy-seven signatures on the petition to Governor John Roberts. .
2 CONT Hans Detweiler is listed among the first settlers of Berks and Philadelphia counties (now Montgomery Co.), naturalized between 1729 and 1730 [Section II of the Appendix, page 434, in Rupp's Index to the 30,000 Names of Immigrants 
2 CONC in Pennsylvania, 1727-1776] together with 83 other inhabitants of Philadelphia Co., most of these probably being from the area now known as Montgomery Co. Since all English immigrants were British subjects, only immigrants of Euro
2 CONC pean extraction, many of them Mennonites, were required to take the oath of allegiance or become natrualized. Hans was a weaver by trade and records show he owned land and paid quit-rent prior to 1734. The annual audit book, "The 
2 CONC Skippack Alms Books," of the Mennonite Church was signed twice by Hans between 1750 and 1757. .
2 CONT Hans and Susanna Detweiler had their homestead at Skippack (which was then called Bebber's Township) from the 1720's until their deaths. It is thought that this property is in Perkiomen Twp., between the Skippack and Perkiomen Cre
2 CONC eks at the head of a small stream between the present route 29 and route 113, not far from Rahn's Station. On the 1848 Montgomery Co. map it is 40 degrees 13' latitude and 0 Degrees 36' longitude and marked by J. Bean across from 
2 CONC the H. Tyson farm. Between 1737 and 1752 Hans Detweiler bought and sold various properties around Bedminister Twp., Bucks Co. According to Edna Gehman, the old Detweiler homestead was in Bebber's Twp., Philadelphia Co., which is n
2 CONC ow Skippack Twp., Montgomery Co. It was used as a meeting house before the Franconia meeting house was built. .
2 CONT The exact dates of death of Hans and Susanna Detweiler is not known, nor is the burial site although most gererally agree that they are buried at the Lower Skippack Mennonite Cemetery. Hans Detweiler's will was made 17 December 17
2 CONC 50, and it mentions his wife by name, Susanna Tatwiler. Since no new will was made between 1750 and 1761, the year of Han's death, it is probable that Susanna was still living at that time. This will was probated 26 Jun 1761 and i
2 CONC s located at the Orphan's Court, County of Philadelphia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information by Susan May Hunsicker - Find a Grave Contributor
