1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/39789391/anna-maria-alderfer
2 CONT
2 CONT Alderfer, Anna (Detweiler), b. 1712, d. 18 Jan 1767, age: 55yr, s/w Frederick Alderfer, [JH]
2 CONT
2 CONT The following is from the "Opportunity and Conscience" exhibit, Mennonite Heritage Center, Harleysville, PA:
2 CONT
2 CONT Precious little is known about the experience of Mennonite girls and women in 18th century Pennsylvania.  But the story of Anna Detweiler Clemmer Alderfer is better documented than most.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Detweiler was born in 1712, probably in the Palatinate, to Mennonites Hans (John) and Susanna Detweiler.  At the age of five, probably in 1717, she immigrated to Pennsylvania with  her parents and perhaps two siblings.  Befor
2 CONC e long, they settled in Bebber's Township, Philadelphia County (now Skippack Township, Montgomery County), where her parents purchased a farm of 102 acres in 1724.  Hans Detweiler was a weaver by trade, and Anna would eventually h
2 CONC ave four brothers and two sisters.  The family was a part of the Skippack Mennonite congregation.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1731, Anna married Hans Clemmer, a Mennonite immigrant of 1730, likely also from the Palatinate.  They soon settled in Salford Township (now Lower Salford) and by 1734 had begun to acquire two tracts of land totaling 156 acr
2 CONC es.  In August, 1733, Hans Clemmer went down to the docks in Philadelphia and contracted with a British ship captain for a young immigrant, an 18 year old Lutheran boy named Friedrich Altorfer (Alderfer), and he became their inden
2 CONC tured servant for several years.  During these years, Hans and Anna Clemmer had two daughters, Susanna and Catharine.    Around the time Frederick Alderfer completed his term of service, his master Hans Clemmer died unexpectedly, 
2 CONC at a young age, in the Fall of 1737.  The widow Anna was left with two young children, a plantation of over 150 acres (though not paid for), and the recently released servant Alderfer.
2 CONT
2 CONT So it happened that Anna Detweiler Clemmer married the 23 year-old Frederick Alderfer early in 1738.  Within a year or two, Alderfer would acquire Clemmer's former land holdings and become a member of the Salford Mennonite congreg
2 CONC ation.  In addition to Anna's two daughters, Frederick and Anna would have six more children together.  From them are descended all the Alderfer's and many other families in the area.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Detweiler Clemmer Alderfer, the immigrant girl of 1717, died in January, 1767, at age 55, and was buried in the Salford Mennonite cemetery.  Her second husband Frederick Alderfer survived her by 34 years.  Anna's original gra
2 CONC vestone, shown here, reads simply "AN x AD 1767"
2 CONT
2 CONT Wayne Detwiler
