1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/76967192/isaac-kolb
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC (STRONG) VAN SINTERN4 KOLB BISHOP (JACOB3, DIELMAN2, HENRICH1) (#441) was born in Skippack Twp, Montg. Co., Pa. 28 March 1711. ISAAC died 23 July 1776 in N. Wales, Montgomery Co. Pa., at age 65.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married twice. He married GEERTRAUTA ZIEGLER 11 July 1734 in N. Wales, Montgomery Co. Pa.. (GEERTRAUTA ZIEGLER is #1450.) GEERTRAUTA was born circa 1713 in Montgomery Co. PA, USA. GEERTRAUTA was the daughter of MICHAEL ZIEGLER 
2 CONC REV. and CATHERINE. He married MARGARET KOLB (widow CUNRAD) after 1764 in N. Wales, Montgomery Co. Pa.. (MARGARET KOLB (widow CUNRAD) is #8475.) MARGARET was born 04 October 1723. She married CORNELIUS CUNRAD date unknown. MARGARE
2 CONC T died 09 May 1809 in N. Wales, Montgomery Co. Pa., at age 85.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Heinrich Funk died in 1760 he was replaced, in the office of bishop, by "Strong Isaac" Kolb of Rockhill. Isaac moved from hilly Rockhill to a flat area of Gwynedd in 1764, in the years when a new congregation was gathering at
2 CONC  Plains.
2 CONT
2 CONT A request in Dutch, by four leaders at Krefeld and Utrecht in Europe, was answered in Gennan on March 1, 1773, by three Mennonite Bishops- the brothers-in-law, Isaac Kolb, Andrew Ziegler and Christian Funk the younger.
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC KOLB, son of Jacob Kolb, an immigrant from Europe and son-in-law of Andreas Ziegler, was a pioneer minister in the Skippack, Pa., congregation. He became the first resident minister and bishop in the Rockhill congregation, B
2 CONC ucks County, Pennsylvania. He was ordained to the ministry in 1743 and in 1761, he was ordained a bishop. It was on his farm that Rockhill Church was built. In 1773, together with Christian Funk and Andreas Ziegler, three prominen
2 CONC t leaders in the Franconia Conference district, they wrote a detailed report of the Mennonite churches in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Part of an Address at the Annual Kulp Reunion in 1931 by John D. Souder, Noted Historian of Telford, Pennsylvania. Published in 1936 by a Committee of Descendants of Dielman Kolb, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bishop Isaac Kolb was the son of Pioneer and Deacon Jacob Kolb of Skippack. He was the first ordained preacher of the Mennonite Congregation of Rockhill, Bucks County. Penna. The information we have in this respect concerning him
2 CONC  is from a letter signed by him. Andreas Zeigler and Christian Funk, dated March 1. 1773. to the Brotherhood in Crefeld, Germany, in which letter he states he was ordained preacher in 1744 and "confirmed." which means to the offic
2 CONC e of Bishop. He settled in Rockhill, located in Freetown in the Manor of Perkasie.  
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 20, 1736. Jacob Kolb of Skippack sold to his son. Isaac Kolb. 150 acres of land for which Isaac paid 90 pounds. On this tract is now located the town of Telford. The deed was witnessed by Michael Zeigler and Jacob Soude
2 CONC r. The first deed has attached the "great seal of William Penn." Two days before this transaction took place, Jacob Kolb also sold 150 acres of land to his son-in-law. Michael Derstine, husband of Agnes Kolb. These two young marri
2 CONC ed couples were the first settlers in the reputed Perkasie Manor in Rockhill Township.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1744. Isaac Kolb was ordained a preacher, and Michael Derstine a deacon. thus becoming the first officials of the Rockhill congregation. They erected the first house of worship in 1744. It was a log church and stood on the boun
2 CONC dary line of their several tracts. The brick church of today is the fourth house of worship on this tract. The log church stood until 1776 when an addition was built to it. This second building stood until 1838.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Preacher Isaac Kolb was ordained bishop to take the place of Bishop Heinrich Funk who had died. In 1776. Bishop Isaac Kolb died and in his stead was ordained preacher Christian Funk. son of Heinrich Funk of Franconia. Bishop Chris
2 CONC tian Funk in his "Mirror For All Mankind" mentions Bishop Isaac Kolb several times in an account concerning some difficulties in the Church. The Revolutionary War was coming on and what the trouble was he did not disclose. as Bish
2 CONC op Isaac Kolb died before the trouble of Funk ripened.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Bishop Isaac Kolb married Gertrude Zeigler. daughter of preacher Andreas Zeigler of Skippack. He resided on the Rockhill tract until January 16. 1764. when he sold the homestead to preacher Samuel Bechtel of Saucon. Northampton Co
2 CONC unty. Pa. for 900 pounds. It was known as the "preachers' homestead." six preachers having resided on the same farm since then.        It appears that Bishop Isaac Kolb's wife died and the next we hear of him is in Gwynedd Townshi
2 CONC p, Philadelphia County. in the region in which Lansdale is now located. It is quite probable that he was then affiliated with "The Plain" Congregation near that place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abstract of will of ISAAC KOLB   Dated 7 August 1772 Proved August 7th. 1776.  Recorded in Deed Book Q.. page 325. Philadelphia.
2 CONT
2 CONT To my beloved Wife Margaret. all she brought from her first husband Cornelius Conrad. and the interest of 200 pounds. yearly, My estate of 134 acres is to be sold. My estate is to be divided to all of my children: Catherine wife o
2 CONC f Samuel Moyer. Jacob, Children of Michael Kolb. Isaac. Elizabeth wife of Jacob Alderfer. Dielman. Susan. Gertrude. and Hannah. My son Isaac Kolb. and son-in-law Samuel Meyers. Executors. Witness my hand and seal the 7th day of Au
2 CONC gust. 1772.
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC KOLB (SEAL)
2 CONT
2 CONT WITNESS: Michael Zeigler Margaret Weber Andrew Zeigler
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac Kolb lived on the "preachers' homestead" in Rockhill. Bucks County. from 1735-6 to 1764. when it appears his wife died. Soon after he married widow Cornelius Conrad. and moved to the Gwynedd section. Where he and his wife ar
2 CONC e buried is not known, He was an active church leader. instrumental in bringing settlers to the Rockhill community. and organizing a congregation. thus helping to make this land a "New Goshen" for his brethern.
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC KOLB, was an extremely strong man, and was known as "Der Grosse Isaac" or "der sehr starke man" the very strong man. Incidents were recalled by Dr. John Overholt. "The Kulp ancestors were a strong, athletic people, some of t
2 CONC hem almost giants. It is related that Isaac Kulp (known as " Strong Isaac ") was a powerful man. It is said that when he was building a house, one day while at dinner, he was discussing with his workmen whether the cross beams cou
2 CONC ld he hauled to the place with two horses, or whether four horses would he required. After dinner he walked out to where the timber lay, and shouldered it, and to the utter surprise of the workmen they saw him bearing the piece of
2 CONC  timber on his shoulders. The fame of his strength was heralded far and wide throughout the neighborhood round about. He was a peaceably disposed man, and would not fight, though often challenged. When on a certain trip to Philade
2 CONC lphia, he stopped at a hotel over night, he came in contact with a bully, who challenged him to fight, which was declined, whereupon the bully branded him a coward and struck him in the face, which angered Mr. Kulp so that he grab
2 CONC bed the bully by both his arms near the shoulders and set him in a chair that squashed under him. When he arose from the floor both of his arms were powerless. The bully said he was satisfied with the experiment, and did not wish 
2 CONC to fight with Kulp after that.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1800 two of Isaac Kolb's daughters emigarated to Canada with the large group headed by John Fretz. They were Hannah married to Mannaseh Fretz and Susanna married to Jacob Housser.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION - KOLB, KULP or CULP FAMILY by Daniel Kolb Cassel. published in 1895 by Morgan R. Wills in Norristown Pa.
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC (STRONG) VAN SINTERN KOLB BISHOP and GEERTRAUTA ZIEGLER had the following children:
2 CONT
