1 NOTE 1. 71. MARTIN RITTENHOUSE (NICHOLAS5, WILLIAM DEWEES4, NICHOLAS (CLAES)3, WILLEM2 RÜDDINGHUYSEN, CLAES1 RETTINGHAUSEN) (#21753) was born in Roxborough Twsp, Phil. Co., Pa. 12 February 1747. MARTIN died before 20 January 1829 in Ro
2 CONC xborough Twsp, Phil. Co., Pa., at age 81. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married SUSANNA KOLB DETWEILER 05 December 1769. (SUSANNA KOLB DETWEILER is #22495.) SUSANNA was the daughter of JOSEPH DETWEILER and MARIA KOLB . 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARTIN RITTENHOUSE was a miller by trade and lived in Roxborough Township, Philadelphia County, Pa. He did not own any property in Roxborough Township so must have been working for someone else. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He died late in 1828 Intestate and his estate was administered by his son JACOB. The probate in the Orphan's Court in Norristown. Pa. disclosed a surprising bit of information. The petition to the Court on 20 January 1829 outlined
2 CONC  all the heirs to the estate including sons NICHOLAS, JACOB and MARTIN. Also daughters ELIZABETH wife of Thomas Umsted, SARAH wife of Phillip Reiner and SUSAN wife of Daniel Nixon. But the surprise was that the petiton said he had
2 CONC  another son JOSEPH who in 1793 had married MARY KNERR and they had 3 children, SUSAN, MARY and JOSEPH but about 1804 he had deserted his family and had never been heard from again. So the petition clarified the heirs who had a cl
2 CONC aim and asked that a jury be appointed by the Court to evaluate land that MARTIN RITTENHOUSE owned in Limerick township, Montgomery County, Pa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This property contained about 106 acres and was located on the Perkiomen and Reading turnpike and bounded by lands of George Boyer, Henry Hunsicker and Jacob Printzman. By law when a possible heir was not present, the Administrato
2 CONC rs were required to place an advertisement in 2 local newspapers which in this case were The Norristown Register and The Democratic Press. The Court ordered the Sheriff to form a jury to evaluate the land, Sheriff Jones Davis had 
2 CONC to choose "twelve free, honest and lawful men". This was called an "inquisition" and they actually viewed the land in question. The men chosen were Adam Printzman, Peter Longaker, Isaac Linerman, John Gross, Johan peterman, P. Gil
2 CONC bert, George Wiedner, Conrad Fryer,George Beck, George Snyder and W. Binghust. They reported the value of the land as $30 per acre. Another of their jobs was to give their opinion as to whether the land could be divided fairly amo
2 CONC ng the heirs without jeopardizing the value. They advised this could not be done as there were too many heirs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Court in cases like this gives the Sheriff the order to sell the land at public auction after giving the "exposure and outcry" of the time and place the auction would be held. This was to be on 7 November 1829. The highest bid
2 CONC der was David Hartranst and he took the land for $2,751. 00.. This was affirmed by the Administrators of the estate on 16 November 1829. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There is no distribution list in the file so we can assume this amount was divided equally among the heirs.There is also no further mention of the missing JOSEPH D. RITTENHOUSE so he may have died or preferred not to appear after 
2 CONC deserting his family 25 years earlier. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orphan's Court file is OR 15872 and is held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pa. 
1 CHAN
2 DATE 01 MAY 2022
3 TIME 22:48:33
