1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/74495379/abraham-gotwals
2 CONT
2 CONT ABE GOTWALS PASSES AWAY In the death of Abe Gotwals at Hyattville last Sunday night. Wyoming and Big Horn county lost one of the early pioneers who was influential in the upbuilding of this country. Mr. Gotwals, familiarly known a
2 CONC s "Abe," came to Wyoming in 1869 and settled in Big.Horn county in 1882. He took up a claim near where Hyattville is now located, where he has resided ever since, and has taken an active interest in the growth of the country. He w
2 CONC as one of the veterans of the Civil war and has evidenced true patriotism in all his ideas at this time. He was anxious to enlist this time, but his age and condition would not permit him to do so. About a week before he died he w
2 CONC as taken ill and went to bed for a few days to get his blood poison straightened out. While confined to his bed, his rupture broke loose and, after exerting every effort to control it, died Sunday night at about midnight. He was c
2 CONC onscious to the last and bore his pain with great fortitude and bravery. The condition of his body would not permit his relatives to hold the funeral Tuesday so they decided that Monday afternoon, in spite of the fact that many [n
2 CONC oon, in spite of the fact that they [sic]] would not be able to hear about his death. The sermon was preached by the Rev. R. O. Gaither at the hall in Hyattville, who spoke highly of the qualities of citizenship displayed in his l
2 CONC ife. He was born in Pennsylvania in 1841, on June 4th, and at the time of his death was just past 76. He lived as a bachelor until about four or five years ago, when he was married to Mrs. Dow Waln.
