1 NOTE {geni:about_me} January 19, 1939 - Vol. XXXI, No.42                     
2 CONT
2 CONT Reesor -- Lydia Ann Weber was born at Waterloo, Ont., May 11, 1891; died Dec. 29, 1938, at Markham, Ont.; aged 47 y. 7 m. 18 d. Sister Reesor was a daughter of Joseph S. and the late Mary Ann (Shantz) Weber of Waterloo. In 1905 du
2 CONC ring a revival held by Bro. A. D. Wenger, she confessed the Lord as her Saviour and the same year was baptized and became a member of the Mennonite Church at Waterloo. On Dec. 20, 1916, she was married to Bro. Joseph B. Reesor of 
2 CONC Markham. To this union 9 children were born (Mary, Lewis, Martha, Annie, Eva, David, John, Esther, and Ruth). Seven of these have confessed Jesus as their Saviour and are members of the church. She also leaves an aged father (livi
2 CONC ng in Saskatchewan), 7 brothers (Allan, Joseph, and Byron of Waterloo; Lewis S., missionary to Argentina, S. A.; Newton of Ft. Wayne, Ind., mission; Orphen of Baden, Orval of London), and 3 sisters* Mrs. A. R. Burkholder of Markha
2 CONC m, Mrs. Herbert Snyder of Waterloo, Mrs. Leonard Snyder, Waterloo, Mrs. Delton Schmitt of Waterloo). One sister (Mrs. Nelson Martin) died a number of years ago while they had charge of Toronto Mission. There also remains a sorrowi
2 CONC ng mother-in-law (Mrs. Tillman Reesor of Markham), who feels very keenly the loss of a loving daughter who kindly cared for her. The church at Markham, also the surrounding community, is grieved because of the loss of a Christian 
2 CONC mother who was so much needed in the home, but we humbly yield as she also did before her passing and say with her, "The Lord's will be done." She had not been in good health for some time, and less than a week before her passing 
2 CONC developed a slight attack of pneumonia. Having a weak heart, the two conditions proved too much for her and she triumphantly passed away. The same day she requested her family to sing for her at the bedside. She helped to sing the
2 CONC  chorus of the Sweet Bye and Bye." The one comforting thought of the sorrowing husband, family, church circle, and the community is the knowledge that a loving mother has entered the courts above to be with her Lord. Funeral servi
2 CONC ces were held Jan.1 at the home by Bro. A. D. Grove, Bro. Cockburn of the Congregational Church and Bro. Oscar Burkholder of Breslau who preached the sermon. Text, Jno. 11:25, 26.
2 CONT
2 CONT *Note: This obituary says "...3 sisters", but four sisters are listed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Stouffville Tribune (Stouffville, ON), 5 Jan 1939, p. 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother of Nine
2 CONT     Called by Death
2 CONT      _______ Mrs. Jos. Reesor of Markham
2 CONT   Township, Succumbed to Sud-
2 CONT   den Illness in 48th Year-
2 CONT   Member of Mennonite Faith
2 CONT   and Highly Esteemed
2 CONT         ___________
2 CONT   The whole Markham Mennon- ite district was stirred with grief last week when the news spread about that Mrs. Joseph Reesor had succumbed to an attack of pneumonia about five o'clock on the evening of Dec.  29. Despite all the do
2 CONC ctor and a  trained nurse could do her heart would not stand the strain of even a mild attack of pneu- monia. Mrs. Reesor was only in her 48th year and leaves be- sides her husband nine children the youngest being eight years.
2 CONT   Born at Waterloo Mrs. Reesor was a daughter of Joseph and Mary Ann Weber. As a young woman she came to Markham to be with her sister Mrs. Louis* Burkholder and here she met  her future husband. They were married on Dec. 20, 1916
2 CONC , thus did she pass away just a few days after her 22nd wedding anniversary. Besides her sor- rowing husband the nine child- ren are: Mary, Lewis, Martha, Annie, Eva, David, John, Esther and Ruth. There is also in the home on the 
2 CONC 8th con- cession of Markham the elderly mother of Mr. Reesor, (Mrs.  Tilman Reesor,) who is in her 79th year, but she has been con- fined to bed for three weeks and could not attend the funeral of her daughter-in-law. There are  a
2 CONC lso four sisters and seven brothers surviving the mother who is gone, besides her aged father and step-mother.
2 CONT   The funeral on Sunday after- noon was very largely attended.  Following hte short service at the late home taken by Louis Burkholder the public service in the church was in charge of  Aaron Grove assisted by S.H. Cockburn of the
2 CONC  Christian church, Ringwood. Rev. Oscar Burkholder of Bresleau (sic) preach- ed the sermon.
2 CONT   The late Mrs. Reesor accepted Christ at a very definite date in her life. She united with the Mennonite church in March 1905, and all who knew her could testify to the beautiful and faithful christian life she led.  The one comf
2 CONC orting thought of the sorrowing husband and large family is the knowledge that a beloved mother has sure- ly entered into that larger life among the saints in Glory. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Probably should read Mrs Amos Burkholder - not Mrs. Loius Burkholder
2 CONT ** Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=19684439 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 10 2016, 19:34:26 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
