1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Among the early immigrants was the progenitor of our branch of the Overholt family, Marcus Oberholtzer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT As part of this migration, Marcus and his wife Elizabeth made their way to Germany, then to Holland. Here, they soon found themselves to be refugees in Queen Anne's War. Their only recourse then was to turn their heads west -- to 
2 CONC the new world, America. Within one year, more than 6,000 set sail. Within this exodus, seven Oberholtzer families, likely related, came to America in the early 18th century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus and his family were in a group of 852 refugees from the Palatinate who were taken to London in a troopship departing Holland April 1709, arriving on May 3rd. The family remained in England for more than a year before travel
2 CONC ing on. This was Marcus, his wife Elizabeth and five children: Jacob, Samuel, Nanny, Marcus and Elizabeth. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family eventually arrived in America 23 September 1710 on the ship Mary Hope. Soon after reaching America they settled on a 500 acre tract on the west bank of the Schuylkill River in Pennsylvania, across from Pottstown in pres
2 CONC ent East Coventry Township, Chester County. The family prospered. The oldest list of settlers in Coventry Township were the tax rolls of 1718 which lists Marcus Overhult. He appears again in 1724. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus died in 1724. Some of his sons held the farm in Coventry, while others migrated to Plumstead Township, Bucks County in 1725. Jacob, the oldest son is known to have farmed south of Deep Run Mennonite church there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marcus and Elizabeth had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT    Jacob Oberholtzer	 1699-1760  m. Barbara Fretz
2 CONT
2 CONT    Samuel Oberholtzer	 1700-1759  m. Elizabeth ?
2 CONT
2 CONT    Nanny Oberholtzer	 1703-1787  m. Jacob Wismer
2 CONT
2 CONT    Marcus Oberholtzer	 1706-1760
2 CONT
2 CONT    Elizabeth Oberholtzer 1707-?	    m. a Kolb
2 CONT
2 CONT    Martin Oberholtzer	 1709-1744  m. Agnes Kolb
2 CONT
2 CONT    Henry Oberholtzer	 1712-1763  m. Elizabeth Killian
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT An Overholt Family Introduction:
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1600s in Europe were troubled times as religious persecution caused thousands of Protestants to flee from their homes. Central Europe was a scene of a series of wars with intermittent years of harassment. Much as it is today i
2 CONC n the Balkans. the oppressed people of France, Switzerland and southern Germany became refugees from their ancestral lands. Forced to find new homes where they could live in peace, vast numbers of families left for America by way 
2 CONC of Holland and England sometime in the mid-seventeenth century. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The causes responsible for forcing these people to leave their homes, possessions, friends and associates are historically clear. Simply stated, the Catholic majority was determined to stamp out all seeds of rebellion against the 
2 CONC Roman Catholic church. Life was made so difficult in central Europe in the late sixteenth and seventeenth centuries for Huguenots (Protestants), Quakers and Mennonites (Anabaptists) that thousands fled for their lives. Many of tho
2 CONC se who escaped down the Rhine were sheltered in temporary camps in Holland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oberholtzer family originated in the Swiss village of Oberholtz, about 30 miles south of Zurich, before the 15th century. As an example of the troubles, records of the village of Oberholtz reveal that on March 2nd, 1661, Marx 
2 CONC (Marcus) Oberholtzer (son of Martin, probably an ancestor) is recorded to have attended an evening Mennonite church service which was "visited" (raided?) by the church authorities. The people were fined and were told that they cou
2 CONC ld no longer hold meetings in their homes under penalty of losing them. It was probably no coincidence that later in that same year, exit permits were issued for Marx and for Jagla (Jacob) with wife and four children, giving them 
2 CONC permission to leave Switzerland for the Palatinate in Germany. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Mennonite Swiss, with other protestant (or non-Catholic) groups in central Europe found temporary refuge in Holland and England. The large numbers of refugees created a massive social problem for the host countries, and a plac
2 CONC e had to be found for these people to live and support themselves. So it was that the significance of the availability of the industrious German farmers was not lost upon wealthy land speculators who possessed patents for large tr
2 CONC acts of land in the new world, America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From their refugee camps in England, the first boatloads left for the new world in 1710. Landing in Philadelphia, the new Americans were first settled on 10,000 acres of land in a newly-opened tract in today's Lancaster County. It
2 CONC  was their good fortune that this land grant was (and still is) some of the most productive farmland in America, a perfect complement to the hard working German farmers. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The flow of Mennonites to Pennsylvania increased steadily over the following years. As land in Lancaster County became fully taken up, other counties were opened, and Mennonite settlements sprang up in Bucks, Franklin, Somerset an
2 CONC d York Counties. Thus, the ethnic "Pennsylvania German" became established in America.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Martin OBERHOLZER 
2 CONT
2 CONT ABT. 1666 - BEF. 1733
2 CONT
2 CONT BIRTH: ABT. 1666 [8851] 
2 CONT
2 CONT DEATH: BEF. 1733, Manor Twp, Lancaster Co PA [8852] 
2 CONT
2 CONT EVENT: Immigrated: 23 SEP 1710, on the ship Maria Hope [8853] 
2 CONT
2 CONT REFERENCE: Hack/Ruth 
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Marx OBERHOLZER 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Margreth DOBLER 
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1 : 
2 CONT
2 CONT +Jacob OBERHOLTZER 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Martin and his brother Michael were on the ship "Maria Hope" which sailed from London and arrived in Philadelphia on September 23, 1710.  By 1725 Martin had purchased 200 acres along the Conestoga Creek and remained there until Ma
2 CONC y 14,1733.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Immigrated Circa 1709 from Switzerland
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1665 in Baden, Germany Death: 17 SEP 1726 in Coventry Twp, Chester Co, PA Note: Arrived in America in about 1710 at age 45, possibly aboard the ship "Ma ry Hope" on 23 Sep 1710.
2 CONT
2 CONT NB: Barbara Ford, genealogist of the Overholser Family Assn states tha t, while a relationship between Marcus and Jacob is suspected, none has be en proven. Marcus is the known son of Marx, not Jacob.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to "Emigrants, Refugees and Prisoners" by Richard Warren Davi s, 1995, Marcus/Mark settled on 300 acres in Coventry Twp, Chester C o, PA sometime before 1711, which he bought from William Fairman.
