1 NAME Martin /Oberholtzer/
2 GIVN Martin
2 SURN Oberholtzer
2 _MARNM Oberholtzer
2 NICK Overholt
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Deep Run East Mennonite Church
2 ADDR
3 CITY Perkasie
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 5 APR 1709
2 ADDR
3 CITY Frankfurt-am-Main
3 CTRY Heiliges Römisches Reich Deutscher Nation
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 NOV 1744
2 PLAC Deep Run
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bedminster Township
3 STAE Pennsylvania
1 OCCU Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Tombstone location in Deep Run Mennonite Cemetery East: Section 2 (South), Row 2, Grave 17.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Overholt, born in the Rhenish Palatinate in 1709, was one of the thousands who were compelled by religious persecutions and the virulence of Franco-German warfare to forsake their native land in the 
2 CONT
2 CONT early part of the 18th century. The exact date of his arrival is not known, but it must have occurred soon after his majority in 1730. That he accompanied his fellow refugees to the recognized meeting place, at  Germantown, may be
2 CONC  safely assumed. But presently he passed on to Bucks County on the Delaware, acquired a farm apparently by lease, in Bedminster Township, married in 1736, died in 1744, in his 36th year, and was buried in the Mennonite Graveyard, 
2 CONC leaving a son, Henry, born in 1739.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 682. Martin Oberholtzer, born 1709 in Germany; died April 05, 1744. He married 683. Agnes Kolb November 02, 1736.
2 CONT
2 CONT 683. Agnes Kolb, born April 18, 1713; died February 02, 1786. She was the daughter of 1366. Henry Kolb and 1367. Barbara ?Fretz.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Martin Oberholtzer and Agnes Kolb are:
2 CONT
2 CONT i. Martin Oberholtzer
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. John Oberholtzer
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. Maria Oberholtzer
2 CONT
2 CONT 341 iv. Barbara Oberholtzer, born November 20, 1737; died May 08, 1823 in Bedminster, Bucks Co., PA; married Christian Fretz 1757.
2 CONT
2 CONT v. Henry Oberholtzer, born February 05, 1738/39; died December 05, 1813; married Anna Butler January 03, 1765.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lots of good information about the Overholts in Germany here:  http://www.karensbranches.com/OberholtzerSites/Germany.html
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT NOTE: The discussion below credited to Betty May contains information culled from the 1903 work of Rev. A.J. Fretz titled "A Genealogical Record of the Descendants of Martin Oberholtzer: Together with Historical and Biographical S
2 CONC ketches and Illustrated with Portraits and Other Illustrations." (Milton, N.J.: The Press of Evergreen News, 1903). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Please also note that the Martin in the top paragraph of the the information below is the father of the Martin Jr in the second paragraph below. It is thought that Martin Sr's headstone (shown here) is a modern replacement for an 
2 CONC earlier stone where the name of "Oberholtzer" was used (the surname being changed to the more commonly used and 'Americanized' version for various reasons). The discussion of Martin Jr outlines reasons for this change. ~ Updated b
2 CONC y Carl Christensen October 2011.
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Oberholtzer (Overholt) Date of Birth 1709 at Frankfort-on-the-main (Now known as Frankfort) Germany. Married Agnes (Maiden Name unknown) on November 2, 1736. She was born April 18, 1713. They were married only eight years a
2 CONC nd had five children before his death on April 5, 1744 One child died in infancy. The four remaining children were: Barbara, Henry, Marie and Martin Jr.  Agnes married a second time to William Nash and was his third wife. They had
2 CONC  four more children. He died in 1760. Agnes died February 15, 1786 and is buried in the same cemetery where Martin is buried. Deep Run Mennonite Cemetery East.
2 CONT
2 CONT Martin Oberholtzer Jr. and other family members changed their last name to the more English version of the name, Overholt. He was born December 20, 1743 in Bucks Co., PA. He married Esther Fretz in 1770. She was the daughter of Ch
2 CONC ristian Fretz who had come to this country around 1720 along with two brothers from Baden, Germany. Martin and Esther had four children by the time the Revolutionary War broke out and fled to Canada for protection. They did not li
2 CONC ke the cold winters in Canada and moved back to Bucks Co. PA when the war was over. In 1810 Martin traveled to Ohio and purchased land in Coshocton and Tuscarawas Countied intending to move his large family (He now had forteen chi
2 CONC ldren) to Ohio. About six weeks before they were to make the move, he bled to death from a cancer on the neck. Esther took her large family and moved to Tuscarawas Co. Ohio.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information supplied by Betty May (Overholt descendent)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Martin Oberholtzer's father was Marcus "Mark" Oberholtzer of 1664 to 1726.
2 CONT
2 CONT Info supplied by Ralph Brown.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Martin Oberholtzer ("senior") are: Barbara Heinrich (aka Henry)- see link below Maria John  Martin, Jr
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT Aug 30, 2016 7:41 PM - Findagrave User #47484531 claims that Martin's birth date is April 5, 1709 but provides no source for this information. 
2 CONT -----------------
2 CONT NOTE: That while the general area that Martin is to have emigrated from is now known as Germany, in the early 1700s, it was still a hodgepodge of Principalities that did not always have a common government. So noting his birth as 
2 CONC 'Germany' is problematic.
2 CONT ----------------
2 CONT See Deep Run East Mennonite East Cemetery notes about the differences between each cemetery at 'Deep Run'
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. A. J. Fretz, A Genealogical Record of the Descendants of Martin Oberholtzer (Milton, N.J., 1903; reprinted 1985 by Westmoreland-Fayette Historical Society), pp. 1-2.
