1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [https://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A207358 DAR Ancestor A207358]
2 CONT
2 CONT aka Grof / Graf, Parents currently unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT "About [1748] Jacob Graff built a large grist-mill on the Perkiomen creek, on the site of Perkiomenville. It was in use about one hundred years," per the History of Bucks County by W. W. H. Davis. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In tax records of 1769 and 1774, Jacob had 4 horses and 6 cattle.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 May 1785, Jacob Groff Senior and wife Mary of Upper Salford deeded 200 & 1/2 acres along the Perkiomen Creek to their son Jacob Groff Junior, also of Upper Salford. This land dates to a patent of 21 June 1709 by Thomas and R
2 CONC ichard Penn, proprietors of the Province of Pennsylvania, who had conveyed it to Jacob Groff Senior.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob's wife Maria died in 1794. A widower in 1800, Jacob lived his remaining years with his son Heinrich's family, also in Upper Salford.
2 CONT
2 CONT Old Goshenhoppen Church Cemetery Records compiled in 1974 show there was a Revolutionary War marker for Jacob. Edward Hocker's 1938 transcription of grave stones also indicates a marker. Jacob would have been 61 at the start of th
2 CONC e Revolution. The marker is not there now. However, three of his sons have Revolutionary War markers at their graves in this section of the cemetery.
