2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/63/dd/ce/87/5344486089388b7a/5344486089388b7a_original.jpg?hash=d78df61395da803ad9abf3df5dca07ad8f401981454ef0fbdd048fdda095a6b5.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Henry Reiner Rittenhouse
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE 1. HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE, on 10 April 1841, filed a petition with the Orphan's Court to have a guardian appointed for their 2 minor children under the age of 14 years. They were CHARLES AUGUSTUS RITTENHOUSE and MARY MAGDALENE R
2 CONC ITTENHOUSE. Orphan's Court File OC 15,124. 
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE was farming in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania in 1850. His farm was appraised at $5,000. With him was his wife SARAH RAUDENBUSH and their 2 children, MARY 18 and CHARLES 16. Fou
2 CONC nd in Montgomery County 1850 Census Roll 799 Book 1 Page 294. 
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY, age 52, was still farming in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania in 1860. His wife SARAH RAUDENBUSH, age 57, as well as 2 girls are living on the farm. One is HENRIETTA BROWER (?) age 16 and the other
2 CONC  is ELIZABETH DEEM (?) age 18 but we don't know their relationship. Their 2 children CHARLES and MARY had evidently left home. Found in Montgomery County 1860 Census Roll 1145 Book 1 page 607. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1870 HENRY and his wife SARAH were still farming in the same location. The 1877 Montgomery County Historical Atlas shows their farm just south of Freeland. Their farm is valued at $15,000 and their personal goods at $5,000. So 
2 CONC in the 20 years they had enlarged and improved their property extensively. They needed 3 hired hands to help on the farm. Found in Montgomery County 1870 Census Roll 1379 Book 1 Page 378. 
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE wrote his will on 22 July 1878 and died 7 weeks later on 14 September 1878. He bequeathed to his wife, SARAH RAUDENBUSH, all the household furnishings she needed and also $300 in cash.. He then directed hi
2 CONC s Executors to place $6,000 in a trust fund, with the interest being paid to SARAH. He left $500 to HARRIET RAMBO, wife of MILTON RAMBO. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To equalize the amounts going to his children, he left $1.200 to his daughter MARY, wife of ABEL THOMAS. The balance of his estate was to be invested in a trust fund, with the interest being paid to his son CHARLES for the balance
2 CONC  of his natural life. Evidently he didn't entirely trust CHARLES to use this money wisely. He had a clause stating that if CHARLES did not use the money for the benefit of his family, the Executors were to pay the interest directl
2 CONC y to CHARLES wife. He appointed his son-in-law ABEL THOMAS and HENRY W. KRATZ as Executors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By law at that date a widow was entitled to choose $300 value from her husband's goods, before the distribution of the estate. SARAH did this and on 2 June 1879 listed what she would keep. These lists are interesting as they discl
2 CONC ose what was important to a housewife, such as the kitchen equipment, beds, cupboards and carpets. But in the case of SARAH she also kept 2 swarms of bees. 
2 CONT When the Executors took the inventorey on 2 October 1878, the value was $28,878.80.Tthis included 40 acrs of mill property in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pa. valued at $4,800. Also the 100 acre farm in the same t
2 CONC ownship, valued at $12,000. So the widow, SARAH, and son CHARLES, would receive the interest from the trust funds. SARAH died in 1887. CHARLES married MARY CROLL and they had 5 children so presumably if the Executors thought he wa
2 CONC s spending his money wisely for his family, CHARLES would continue to receive the interest from his father's estate until he himself died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT However from other Orphan's Court documents. File OC #16,062, settling the estate of ABEL THOMAS, husband of MARY RITTENHOUSE, it mentions a dower right of SARAH, widow of HENRY R. RITTENHOUSE, of Upper Providence Township, Montgo
2 CONC mery County, Pa. We know that SARAH was to receive the interest of a dower charge of $3,333.33. This point had arisen because Abel Thomas had left land to his daughter Matilda Thomas Culp and this dower charge had not been cleared
2 CONC  by the Courts and was therefore still outstanding. The auditor for the Thomas estate declared it would have to be paid before the estate could be settled. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The files for HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE are Will RW #5361 and Orphan's Court File OC #15,385. They are both held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pennsylvania. 
