1 NOTE 1. CHARLES A. RITTENHOUSE moved to Coal Township, Northumberland County by 1860. He was a German Reform Minister. With him were his wife MARY CROLL and daughter HANNAH age 2. Found in Northumberland County 1860 Census Roll 1149 Bo
2 CONC ok 1 Page 95; 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1870 CHARLES had moved his family to Minersville, Schuykill County, Pa. He was still preaching as a Minister of the German Reform Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  Found in 1870 Schuylkill County census Roll 1447 Book1 Page 104. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was still a Minister of the Gospel and with his wife MARY was living in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pa. in 1880. Their 5 children were HANNAH (ANNIE) age 28, HENRY R. 19, CHARLES A. 14, WILLIAM E. 9 and DAVID 
2 CONC LEROY age 5. They had originally lived in Norristown. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Found in Montgomery County 1880 Census Roll 1159 Book 1 Page 171. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES RITTENHOUSE, had been receiving the interest from a $6,000 trust fund set up by his father HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE, when he died in 1878. There was a clause in this Will stipulating that if the Executors thought CHARLES w
2 CONC as not spending the money wisely for the care of his family, they were to pay it directly to his wife. Now that we see that CHARLES was a rather itinerant preacher, moving around the country, we can understand his father's concern
2 CONC s. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHARLES RITTENHOUSE died Intestate on 19 December 1909. This meant the final distribution of his father's Will still had to be made. One of the Executors, ABEL THOMAS, husband of CHARLES' sister, MARY also deceased, had died but t
2 CONC he other Executor, HENRY KRATZ was still living so he administered the estate. The total amount to be distributed was $6,472.70. It was divided equally among the 5 children of CHARLES, except the one son, HENRY had died so his sha
2 CONC re was given to his widow, RENA 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Orphan's Court file is OC #15,385 and is held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pa. 
