2 FORM jpg
1 NOTE 1. ELIZABETH FUNK and her sister CATHARINE FUNK traveled to Canada to visit friends. While there she met JOHN FRETZ RITTENHOUSE and they were married 26 February 1828 in Clinton Township, Lincoln County, Ontario. They took a weddi
2 CONC ng trip, traveling back to Bucks County on horseback. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CATHARINE FUNK also married when she was in Canada with her sister ELIZABETH, visiting friends. She married MOSES GROBB. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of ELIZABETH FUNK widow of JOHN FRETZ RITTENHOUSE 
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Herald of Truth November 1887 
2 CONT
2 CONT RITTENHOUSE.-On the 3d of November, in Clinton township, Lincoln county, Ont., of old age and complicated diseases, Elizabeth Rittenhouse, widow of the late John Rittenhouse, who preceded her about six years. Her age was 80 years,
2 CONC  2 months and 13 days. Her maiden name was Funk, and she was born in Hilltown township, Bucks county, Pa., and came to Canada in the year 1827, where she lived until her death. She united with the Mennonite Church in Pennsylvania 
2 CONC and lived a faithful member, strong unto the end in the faith that her Redeemer liveth. She was buried on the 6th at the Moyer burying-ground, where a large number of relatives and friends were assembled to pay their last respects
2 CONC  to a beloved sister and friend. Funeral services by A.K. Honsberger from John 14:1,2. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION 
2 CONT
2 CONT HISTORY of BISHOP HENRY FUNCK by A. J. Fretz, published in 1899 by the Mennonite Publishing Co. of Elkhart, Indiana.  ELIZABETH FUNK is found on page 571. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of John Fretz Rittenhouse in the Herald of Truth July 1881. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rittenhouse. June 8th, in Lincoln Co., Ont., of the infirmities of old age, gradually sinking till the last, Bro. John Rittenhouse, aged 81 years, and 17 days. He was sick about six weeks, and bore his afflictions with Christian f
2 CONC ortitude. He longed for the time of his departure that he might meet Jesus in a better world, and with this hope he fell asleep in peace. He was buried on the 10th. He was a faithful member of the Mennonite Church for many years. 
2 CONC He leaves an aged companion and seven children and many relatives to mourn his departure, but they mourn not as those who have no hope. His life was one of the most exemplary piety. May we all imitate his faithfulness and devotion
2 CONC . 
