2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/d7/7c/bf/84/5344483db22d385d/dij64qur_original.jpg?hash=b7b3814fe16a35dfdbc9b56a109776a960cc7b2c5247b6d0053be94d4d160cb5.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Rev Abraham F. Rittenhouse
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE 1. ABRAHAM F. RITTENHOUSE was born on his father's farm in Vineland at the corner of John Street and Martin Road. In 2003 it is owned by R. S. Short and Sons. After he married CHRISTINA KIENZLE in 1863 they farmed on the next lot 
2 CONC north of his father's farm. In 1871 he was ordained a Minister of the Moyer (First Mennonite )Church in Vineland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHRISTINA KIENZLE emigrated to Canda with her family in 1853 when she was 11 years old. At first the family settled near Dunnville, Ontario but when she was 12 years old, CHRISTINA went to work in Vineland on the farm of Samuel H.
2 CONC  Moyer. As was the Mennonite custom at the time she was virtually adopted into the family as there were no other girls to help the mother. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When CHRISTINA married Abraham F. Rittenhouse she was even given some dower rights among which was the choice of the best cow in the stable, a prize Jersey called "Gentle". It is thought the bible described below might have been p
2 CONC art of her dower. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889 Rev. Abraham Rrittenhouse led a split in the church as he thought his Old Order group was becoming too progressive and modern. Along with Rev. Joseph Wismer and Deacon Abram Culp he led a group of 41 individuals to form a 
2 CONC separate Mennonite Church known as the Wismer Group. They built a new Meeting House near the corner of Highway #8 and Martin Road. Abraham's older brother, Rev. John F. Rittenhouse took over as pastor of the original church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1913 Rev. Abraham Rittenhouse and Christina sold their Vineland farm and retired to the Markham area where 6 of their 11 children were already living. They retained their Old Order Mennonite beliefs. They are both buried in the
2 CONC  Reesor Church Cemetery on concession 10 in Markham. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Family Bible of REVEREND ABRAHAM F. RITTENHOUSE and his wife CHRISTINA KIENZLE has been donated to the Museum of the Twenty at Jordan, Ontario for safekeeping. It was inherited by Murray and Bernard Reesor from their mother An
2 CONC nie Rittenhouse Reesor and grandfather Aaron Rittenhouse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Bible was printed in Reutlinger, Germany in 1854. This city is about 50 miles north of Tuttlingen where CHRISTINA KEITZLE was born. This version of the Bible was translated by Dr. Martin Luther and contained a concordance and 
2 CONC also a Family Register which revealed a great many family details.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of REV. ABRAHAM FUNK RITTENHOUSE 
2 CONT From Gospel of Truth October 1918 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rittenhouse.--Abraham F. Rittenhouse was born in Lincoln Co., Ont., and died at his home at Cedar Grove near Markham, Ont., on Sept. 5, 1918, in his 78th year. Deceased came into the Mennonite Church in early life and was ordained
2 CONC  to the ministry 45 years ago. He was a staunch exponent of the Mennonite faith. With his wife they lived at Vineland until a few years ago, when they moved to York Co., to be with their children. A sorrowing wife, four sons, and 
2 CONC four daughters survive. The funeral on the 8th at the Townline Mennonite Church was largely attended. The service was conducted by C. Gehman and E. Sievenpipher. 
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION 
2 CONT Mr. Larry Rittenhouse, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada 
