2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/b6/c0/70/72/5344483f0a749ba1/1_original.jpg?hash=d2590c52c2d9e0e199456a1987c88f74fea66dcbe15c285ed73016c87b09c788.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
1 NOTE 1. MOSES K. RITTENHOUSE was such a well known philanthropist in the Vineland, Ontario area that in 1911 Harvey M. Gayman compiled much of the information that had been written about Moses and his various philanthropical gifts to t
2 CONC he Vineland area. This was published by the William Briggs Press in Toronto in 1911 under the title Rittenhouse School and Gardens. An article written by Mr. L. E. Fuller in the American Lumberman was reproduced in full. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moses Rittenhouse left his parent's farm in Clinton Township, Lincoln County, Ontario, when he was 18 years old. He arrived in Chicago in April 1864 and his. first employment was in a planing mill. For his services the first week 
2 CONC he received $3.50, but this was raised to $4.50 the second week. He decided he could better himself elsewhere and found a job at the Chicago Morning Post as a "printer's devil". A month of this work and he quickly decided he prefe
2 CONC rred the lumber business so he was able to get a job with the Prehtigo Company which was a large lumber firm with headquarters in Wisconsin. However the job Moses had with this company was at a distribution yard in the north end o
2 CONC f Chicago.  But Moses decided he would have to improve his education so he returned home to Vineland for the winter of 1864 and attended school there. Returning to Chicago in the spring of 1865 he secured a job with McMullen Funk 
2 CONC and Company, a retail lumber firm. A year later Moses was promoted to the management of one of theretail yards. He resigned this job in December of 1866 to gain additional education. All during the spring of 1867 he attended the E
2 CONC astman Business College. After receiving his diploma he obtained his next job at the wholesale lumber firm of B. L. Anderson and Company. His position was bookkeeper and he remained with them for one year. In April 1868 Moses join
2 CONC ed the firm of J. Beidler Bros. Lumber company. During the next 15 years he moved up from salesman to general manager and then to treasurer, which position he held for 12 years. But Moses decided to go out on his own so in April 1
2 CONC 883 he resigned from Beidler but evidently with some regret. He always remained on good terms with them and said the Beidler brothers were the finest business men he had ever known. Moses then made some arrangement with Jessie R. 
2 CONC Embree and on 1 May 1883 opened an office and lumber yard under the name of Rittenhouse and Embree Company. Susan Hunsberger, must have loaned Moses $875.78 on 7 April 1887 because at her death in 1896 she was holding a note for t
2 CONC hat amount from Rittenhouse and Embree. So this would have been 4 years after the firm was started but at a time when they were expanding with branch yards being established It was probably a good investment. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The company of Rittenhouse and Embree expanded very successfully over the next few years . Also Moses Rittenhouse himself became involved in other enterprises connected to the lumber business. He was president of several lumber co
2 CONC mpanies both in Chicago and in other states. He was a director in Chicago Life Insurance company and the Drover's Deposit National Bank. But the people of the Niagara Peninsula remember Moses F. Rittenhouse for the many philanthro
2 CONC pical gifts he made to the district. His support for libraries began when he bought 500 books, 2 sets of encyclopedias, 20 bound periodicals, and subscriptions to 12 magazines to endow a library in the elementary school which he b
2 CONC uilt in 1886 in Vineland Station. Upon the death of Moses F. Rittenhouse in 1915, the trustees of the Rittenhouse School received $20,000 to endow the library and Victoria Hall. This Victoria Hall was origianlly built across the r
2 CONC oad from the school. It was meant for lectures and general entertainment and was officially opened on Christmas Eve 1904. Some 50 years later, when the school and Victoria Hall were expropriated for the Queen Elizabeth Way interch
2 CONC ange at Victoria Avenue, sufficient money was on hand from the Rittenhouse Trust to build a new library in conjunction with tennis courts on First Avenue in Vineland Station. He purchased sufficient land behind the school to start
2 CONC  a formal garden. This was expanded over the years as it became part of the school curriculum. It became a well known tourist attraction. Moses had purchased the original home of David Grobb which was on 90 acres on the Lake Ontar
2 CONC io waterfront. He built a large addition at the front but maintained all of the original house and this can be seen today in 2003. He donated most of this acreage to start an Agriculture Experimental Farm which is still operating 
2 CONC today. There were many more philanthropical acts to the Vineland area by Moses Rittenhouse including land to extend the Vineland Cemetery beside the original Mennonite Burying Ground. When hedied in 1915 he was the first person to
2 CONC  be buried in this new section. Many of these generous gifts to the area are given in more detail in the articles and books shown in the source of information below. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION 
2 CONT VILLAGE OF VINELAND by Barbara F. Coffman in the Canadian-German Folklore Tales of the Twenty Volume 7, 1979.  A TRIBUTE TO MOSES F. RITTENHOUSE by Barbara F. Coffman in the Canadian-German folklore # 1, 1961   RITTENHOUSE SCHOOL 
2 CONC AND GARDENS by Harvey M. Gayman, 1911       MR. LARRY RITTENHOUSE, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada. MRS. PATRICIA HIGH, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada 
