1 NOTE 1. WILLIAM FUNK RITTENHOUSE was born on the family farm in Vineland, Ontario. Like most children on farms he attended school in the winter months. He received his teacher's certificate at age 17. He taught for 14 years but left th
2 CONC is profession for health reasons. 
2 CONT WILLIAM and his wife MARY MOYER, with their one surviving child, ERNEST, moved to Chicago where in 1886 WILLIAM graduated as a medical doctor. He was a professor at several colleges, becoming President of the Jenner Medical Colleg
2 CONC e. 
2 CONT Unfortunately his ill health returned and he was forced to supplement his income by teaching languages and acting as associate editor of a medical journal. 
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM died in Chicago but was buried back in Vineland in the New Vineland Cemetery. Later both his widow MARY MOYER and his only surviving son DR. ERNEST RITTENHOUSE were buried in the same location. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of DR. WILLIAM FUNK RITTENHOUSE 
2 CONT Taken from Gospel Heerald May 1924 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rittenhouse. - Dr. William Funk Rittenhouse was born in Lincoln County, Ontario, Sept. 5, 1852; died at his home in Chicago, Illinois, March 14, 1924; aged 71 y. 6 m. 9 d. In his early years he lived on his father's farm near Vine
2 CONC land, Ontario He was the youngest of a family of twelve children. He attended the public schools during the winter months and later took a teacher's course in St. Catharines, receiving a certificate to teach at the age of seventee
2 CONC n. He taught school for fourteen years, four of which were spent in the Central School of St.Catharines, Ont. On April 2, 1874, he was married to Mary Moyer of Campden, Ontario, who with one son (Dr. Ernest Rittenhouse of Chicago)
2 CONC  survives him. Owing to the death of two of his sons and the confinement of the school room, his health was broken and he began the study of medicine, graduating from the College of Physicians and Surgeons in Chicago in 1886, and 
2 CONC continuing his profession in that city, where he attained prominence in his work, having been professor of a department in the Illinois Medical College and president and professor of Jenner Medical College. Ten years ago his healt
2 CONC h was so impaired that active work was given up, but his mental powers were active and he was given to study and writing. He had a ready command of German, French, Spanish and Italian, and contributed to the Journal of Clinical Me
2 CONC dicine of Chicago. Funeral services were held in Chicago, after which the body was conveyed to Vineland, Ont., where services were held, a large circle of friends and acquaintances being present. Services were conducted by S. F. C
2 CONC offman. Text, John 11:23. Interment in the Vineland Cemetery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION 
2 CONT MR. LARRY RITTENHOUSE, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada. MRS. PATRICIA HIGH, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada 
