1 NAME Martin Bauman /Snyder/
2 GIVN Martin Bauman
2 SURN Snyder
2 _MARNM Snyder
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Erb Street Mennonite Church Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Waterloo
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 BIRT
2 DATE 22 MAY 1849
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 SEP 1916
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT In spite of the fact that Martin's gravestone indicates he was born in 1848, all other sources including the 1911 Canadian Census agree he was born in 1849. As his parents were married Oct 31, 1848, it is extremely unlikely he was
2 CONC  born in that year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT GOSPEL HERALD - Volume IX, Number 28 - October 12, 1916 - Pages 526 & 527 Snyder. - Martin B. Snyder was born May 22, 1849; died at his home in Waterloo, Ont., Sept. 29, 1916; aged 67 y. 4 m. 7 d. In 1870 he was united in matrimon
2 CONC y with Mary Shantz, to which union were born 9 children, 5 sons and 4 daughters, of whom one daughter preceded him in infancy. Bro. Snyder was a faithful member of the Mennonite Church at Waterloo, and will be greatly missed in th
2 CONC e church, as well as in the home and community, but we trust our loss may be his gain and need not mourn as others that have no hope. Funeral services conducted by Bro. Noah Stauffer and Jonas Snider from Rev. 20:6, on Oct. 3, at 
2 CONC the Waterloo Church and the remains were buried at the David Eby Cemetery. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In spite of the fact that Martin's gravestone indicates he was born in 1848, all other sources including the 1911 Canadian Census agree he was born in 1849. As his parents were married Oct 31, 1848, it is extremely unlikely he was
2 CONC  born in that year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT GOSPEL HERALD - Volume IX, Number 28 - October 12, 1916 - Pages 526 & 527 Snyder. - Martin B. Snyder was born May 22, 1849; died at his home in Waterloo, Ont., Sept. 29, 1916; aged 67 y. 4 m. 7 d. In 1870 he was united in matrimon
2 CONC y with Mary Shantz, to which union were born 9 children, 5 sons and 4 daughters, of whom one daughter preceded him in infancy. Bro. Snyder was a faithful member of the Mennonite Church at Waterloo, and will be greatly missed in th
2 CONC e church, as well as in the home and community, but we trust our loss may be his gain and need not mourn as others that have no hope.                                                                     Funeral services conducted b
2 CONC y Bro. Noah Stauffer and Jonas Snider from Rev. 20:6, on Oct. 3, at the Waterloo Church and the remains were buried at the David Eby Cemetery.
