2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-1/myheritage-family-trees?itemId=175669511-1-1747&action=showRecord&indId=externalindividual-09e14b3551e7b73b0186ee01dd3cd375&mrid=842aa0b4122e18f125ec6e27ae078af4 MyHe
2 CONC ritage Family Trees] via father [http://www.geni.com/profile-37876921 Christian (Meier) Meyer] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 27 2015, 20:01:49 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Abraham is identified as a son of Christian Meyer and Barbara [LNU] and is believed to have arrived in Pennsylvania with his family in 1727.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham was a bookbinder. His first wife's name is unknown, his second wife was named Christina. [3][4] Some sources give Christina's last name as Ackerman.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham was the father of :
2 CONT
2 CONT Christian [4]
2 CONT Catharina [4]
2 CONT Anna [4]
2 CONT Barbara [4]
2 CONT Elizabeth [4]
2 CONT Magdalena [4]
2 CONT The listing of children in Abraham's will lacks punctuation and so it is unclear how many daughters he had. Best records that he had five children, but has been in error before. It is assumed in the absence of any evidence to the 
2 CONC contrary that he had one son and five daughters.
2 CONT In 1766, Abraham Meyer, bookbinder of the Borough of Lancaster and Christina his wife sold his property on Queen Street to John Stoneman for 150 pounds. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT His will was made on 8 Mar 1767, naming as heirs his beloved wife Christina and children "Christian, Catharina, Anna Barbara Elizabeth Magdalena". Executors were his trusty friends Jacob Meyer and Samuel Meyer of Manheim. The will
2 CONC  was witnessed by Johannes Schruber, BarnedBrubeck and Casper Shaffner. It was proven on 2 Apr 1767 in Lancaster Boro. [3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes: 
2 CONT In 1742, Abraham Maier of the Township of Hempfield purchased a 107-acre tract of land in the township of Mountjoy (formerly Donegall) from John Smith.[7] In 1748, Abraham Myer (then "of the Borough of Lancaster") sold the land to
2 CONC  Magdalena Witmore for 65 pounds. The 1748 deed was witnessed by Henry Keelhoffer and Valentin Metzler, but it was a certain Jacob Myers who appeared before the Court of Common Pleas in 1786 to affirm "that he was well acquainted 
2 CONC with Abraham Myer the above named Grantor in his lifetime" and that the signature on the deed was Abraham's. [8] Note Bishop Valentine Metzler is reported to have bought the Meyer family bible in 1767, around the same time that Ab
2 CONC raham died, and evidently had a connection with the family. It seems likely that Abraham who had been living in Hempfield and bought the tract of land in Mountjoy was the bookbinder and Valentin Metzler who witnessed the deed was 
2 CONC the same person who later purchased Abraham's bible.
2 CONT
2 CONT While it is assumed that the Meyer family bible, acquired by Bishop Valentine Metzler on 27 April 1767,[9] had formed part of the estate of Abraham Meyer who died earlier that month, the Bible carried a note that it was to be inhe
2 CONC rited by Abraham's son, Christian. It is unclear how it came to belong to Bishop Metzler.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1747, Abraham Myer, George Michael Swob, and Frederick Huber witnessed the will of Jacob Free of Lancaster Boro, Lancaster, PA. When the will was proven, George and Frederick gave their solemn oaths and Abraham his solemn affir
2 CONC mation that they had seen Jacob make the will.[10]
2 CONT ====== Sources
2 CONT 1.  Ancestry.com. Lancaster, Pennsylvania, U.S., Mennonite Vital Records, 1750-2014 [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations, Inc., 2015. Original data: Genealogical Card File. Lancaster Mennonite Historical Soc
2 CONC iety, Lancaster, Pennsylvania. Citing (i) Emigrants, Refugees and Prisoners, II, 293 and (ii) Valentine Metzler Froschauer Bible, F&M College Archives, and (iii) bro Hans Meier (1684-1757) [?]. Viewed at https://www.ancestry.com.a
