1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dielman KOLB was 
2 CONT *born in 1648 in Wolfsheim, Baden, GERMANY. 
2 CONT *He died on 13 Oct 1712 in Manheim, GERMANY. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for DIELMAN KOLB: Little is known of this family, but they must have been people of means and position, for four of their six sons were to become ministers of the Mennonite faith: Peter who remained in Germany; and three of 
2 CONC those who came to America, Henry, Martin and Dielman. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A gate in which Dielman carved his name still survives. It is inscribed "Tilmann Kolb 1699". 
2 CONT
2 CONT More About DIELMAN KOLB: 
2 CONT *Burial: Manheim, GERMANY 
2 CONT
2 CONT More About AGNES SCHUMACHER: 
2 CONT *Burial: Wolfsheim, Palatinate, GERMANY 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of DIELMAN KOLB and AGNES SCHUMACHER are: 
2 CONT
2 CONT *i. PETER KOLB, born 1671 in Manheim, GERMANY; died Bet. 1726 - 1727 in Manheim, GERMANY. 
2 CONT **Notes for PETER KOLB: Peter was a Mennonite minister. He did not follow his brothers to America, but at least one of his sons (Hans Casper) did. Two other Kolbs who came to America are believed to be sons of Peter, Dielman who a
2 CONC rrived in 1729 and Johann Jacob who arrived in 1737. 
2 CONT **More About PETER KOLB: Occupation: Minister Religion: Mennonite 
2 CONT *ii. Ann Kolb, born 1676 in GERMANY; died 26 February 1737/38 in Wolfsheim, GERMANY; married Balthaser Kolb 
2 CONT *iii. Henry Kolb, born 1678 in GERMANY; died Cir. 1730 in Skippack, Montgomery Co., PA. 
2 CONT *iv. Martin Kolb, born 1680; died 1761 in Skippack, Montgomery Co., PA; married Mary Magdalena Van Sintern 19 May 1709; born 1687; died Aft. 1771. 
2 CONT *v. Johannes Kolb, born 19 May 1683 in GERMANY; died in Montgomery Co., PA. 658 
2 CONT *vi. JACOB KOLB, born 21 May 1685 in Wolfsheim, Baden, GERMANY; died 4 October 1739 in Skippack Twp., Montgomery Co., PA; married SARA VAN SINTERN 21 May 1710 in Germantown, Philadelphia Co., PA. 
2 CONT *vii. Dielman Kolb, born 10 November 1691 in Wolfsheim, Baden, GERMANY; died in Montgomery Co., PA; married Elizabeth Schnebli 1714. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dielman Kolb was 
2 CONT *born in 1648. 
2 CONT *He resided in Wolfsheim in Baden, Germany. 
2 CONT *He died in 1712, aged 64 years. 
2 CONT *He is buried at Manheim, Germany. 
2 CONT *Dielman married Agnes Schumacher, daughter of Peter Schumacher who came to America in 1685. Peter Schumacher was a Mennonite, but later united with the Friends. He came from Kriesheim and died in Germantown, Pennsylvania in 1707.
2 CONC  Agnes (Schumacher) Kolb died in 1705, aged 53 years and is buried at Wolfsheim. 
2 CONT *Dielman and Agnes had at least seven children, perhaps ten. A 1685 census in Wolfsheim shows them with 5 sons and 2 daughters. Five or six of the sons came to America and at least two grandsons. Children: 
2 CONT **1. Peter Kolb was born in 1671 and died in 1727. He was a Mennonite minister and Bishop and is buried at Manheim. He did not come to America, but at least two of his sons did and some of his descendants later immigrated to Canad
2 CONC a from Bucks County, Pennsylvania. Some researchers believe that Peter is the father of Hans Casper Kolb who came to America in 1729. #Children1 
2 CONT **2. Ann Kolb was born in 1676, married Balthaser Kolb and died at Wolfsheim February 26, 1738. Ann did not come to America. Henry Kolb was born in 1679 and died in July, 1730. He came to America in 1709 and married Barbara (Fretz
2 CONC ?). He settled first at Germantown and then at Skippack, Montgomery Co. where he was a minister at Skippack Mennonite Church. He died in July, 1730. #Children3 
2 CONT **3. Martin Kolb was born in 1680 and came to America in 1707 where he settled in Germantown and married Magdalena Van Sintern, daughter of Isaac Van Sintern and Neltje (Classen) Sintern and gr-gr-grandaughter of Jan de Voss, Burg
2 CONC omaster in Flanders in 1550. Martin was also a minister of the Mennonite Church serving at Germantown and Skippack where he died in 1761. #Children4 
2 CONT **4. Johannes Kolb was born May 19, 1683 and died in South Carolina in 1765(?). He came to America in 1707 with brothers Martin and Jacob and settled first in Germantown and by 1709 in Skippack. He also owned land in Coventry town
2 CONC ship in Chester Co. He married Sarah (?). Some time after 1734, he moved to South Carolina and received a grant of 650 acres in the Causeway Neck of the Pee Dee River in South Carolina in 1737. #Children5 
2 CONT **5. Jacob Kolb was born May 21, 1685 and came to America in 1707 with brothers Martin and Johannes. He married Sarah Van Sintern, sister of Martin's wife Magdalena, at Germantown May 2, 1710. The ceremony was performed by Dirk Ke
2 CONC yser in the old log Mennonite Church. He was, like his brother Martin, a trustee of the Skippack Mennonite Church. He died October 4, 1739 when the beam of a cider press fell on him and he was fatally injured. #Children6 
2 CONT **6. Arnold Kolb was born in 1688 and came to America in 1709 with Henry Kolb who was probably his brother. Arnold disappeared from the records after his arrival and we do not have any confirmation that he was one of the Kolb brot
2 CONC hers, but it seems likely. 
2 CONT **7. Dielman Kolb was born May 10, 1691 and married Elizabeth Schnebli in 1714. Dielman and Elizabeth came to America from Ibersheim in the Palatinate in 1717. He was one of founders of the Mennonite Church at Salford in Montgomer
2 CONC y Co. Pa. He was one of a committee to arrange and supervise the translation of the "Martyr's Mirror" from the Dutch to the German langauge. #Children7
2 CONT
2 CONT Children were: Johannes KOLB, Jacob KOLB.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrived in Germantown, Pa in 1707.
2 CONT
2 CONT History of the Kolb family beginning with 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 1 1  DIELMAN KOLB, of Wolfsheim, in Baden, Germany. He was born about 1648, and did in 171 2 or 171 3, aged 64 years, and  is buried at Manheim. He married the fifth child, a daughter of Peter Schumacher, who afterwards came to 
2 CONC Germantown in 1685, and died in 1707, aged 85 years. He was a Mennonite, but later united with the Friends. He is the first person definitely ascertained to have come from Kriesheim. (Says S. W. Pennypacker.) He bought 200 acres o
2 CONC f  land from Herman Op den Graeff. The agreement is dated Aug. 16, 1685. The deed is written in the Dutch or Holland language and recorded in the Germantown book. The wife of Dielman Kolb died in 1705, aged 53 years, and is buried
2 CONC  at Wolfsheim, in the Palatinate. Their children were Peter, Ann, Martin, Jacob, Henry, Dielman and Johannes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHILDREN OF No. 1  DIELMAN KOLB 
2 CONT
2 CONT Individual Family No. No. = # in front of names 
2 CONT
2 CONT * 2 2 1  Peter Kolb, b. 1671; d. 1727; buried at Manheim, in the Palatinate, aged 56 years, 8 months. He was a Mennonite minister. 
2 CONT * 2 3 2 Ann Kolb, b. 1676; d. Feb. 26, 1738, at Wolfsheim ; m. Balthaser Kolb. Never came to America. 
2 CONT * 2 4 3 Martin Kolb,b.. 1680. Came to America and settled at Germantown in 1707. Married Magdalena, daughter of Isaac Ven Sintern and Neeltje Claasen, and greatgreat-grand-daughter of Jan de Voss, Burgomaster in Flanders, in 1550.
2 CONC  Martin Kolb moved to Skippack in 1709, and was ordained to the ministry in the Mennonite Church and served at Germaintown, and at Skippack as soon as an organization was established there. His signature also appears on the deed o
2 CONC f the Mennonite Church at Germantown as a witness. He was one of the first trustees, 
2 CONT * with others, who held the property of the Mennonite Church at the Skippack in trust for many years. He died in 
2 CONT * 1761. Their children were Magdalena, Neltgen, Hannah, Sarah, Dielman, Isaac, Maria.
2 CONT * 2 5 4 Jacob,b. May 21, 1685 ; came to America in 1707 and settled at Germantown ; was m. to Sarah Van Sintern (sister to Magdalena, Martin's wife) in the year 1710, May 2d, in the old log Mennonite Church, at Germantown, in pres
2 CONC ence of  the full congregation. Dirk Keyser officiating.  An obituary notice of him says : " On the 4th .instant, October, 1739, Jacob Kolb, of Skippack. As he was pressing cider the beam of the press fell on one side of his head 
2 CONC and shoulder and wounded him so that he languished about half an hour and then died, to the exceeding grief of his relatives and family, who are numerous, and concern of his friends and neighbors, among whom he lived many years in
2 CONC  great esteem." Aged 54 years. He was also a trustee, in connection with his brother, Martin, of Skippack Mennonite congregation as early as 1717, where he had moved in 1709. 
2 CONT * 2 6 5 Henry Kolb b. --- ; d. 1730. He came to. America in 1707 and settled at Germantown; in 1709 he moved to Skippack, now Montgomery county, Pa. He was also a minister in the Mennonite Church at Skippack. His will is dated Feb
2 CONC . 20, 1729, and probated July 13,  1730. His children were: Peter, David, Dielman, Mary, Dorothy, Annie and Agnes. He was also a trustee of the Mennonite Church at Skippack, with his brothers, Jacob Kolb and Martin Kolb. 
2 CONT * 2 7 6 Dielman  Kolb, b. Nov. 10, 1691, in the Palatinate (Pfaltz) at Wolfsheim ; m. Elizabeth Schnebli in 17 14, in his 23d year.  On Mar. 21, 1717, started at Ibersheim, in the Palatinate, for Pennsylvania, in 276 America ; arr
2 CONC ived at Philadelphia on the loth of Aug., 1717. 
2 CONT * 2 7 8 Johannes Kolb, b. May 19, 1683. Came to America in 1707; moved to Skippack, now Montgomery county, Pa., in 1709, and bought 150 acres of land — the deed is dated Dec. 15, 1709. His name also appears on a petition for a roa
2 CONC d to  Farmers Mill in 17 13, and on the tax list in 1734 for 150 acres
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Cassel, Daniel Kolb(, b. 1820-?)  , A genealogical history of the Kolb, Kulp or Culp family: and its branches in America , 1895 Publisher: Norristown, Pa. : Morgan R. Willis  , pg. 16 - 19,483-489. "A genealogical history of the K
2 CONC olb, Kulp or Culp family : and its branches in America, with biographical sketches of their descendants from the earliest available records ...". " History of the Kolb family beginning with 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 1 1  DIELMAN KOLB, of Wolfsheim, in Baden, Germany. He was born about 1648, and did in 171 2 or 171 3, aged 64 years, and  is buried at Manheim. He married the fifth child, a daughter of Peter Schumacher, who afterwards came to 
2 CONC Germantown in 1685, and died in 1707, aged 85 years. He was a Mennonite, but later united with the Friends. He is the first person definitely ascertained to have come from Kriesheim. (Says S. W.  Pennypacker.) He bought 200 acres 
2 CONC of  land from Herman Op den Graeff. The agreement is dated Aug. 16, 1685.  The deed is written in the Dutch or Holland language and recorded in the Germantown book. The wife of Dielman  Kolb died in 1705, aged 53 years, and is bur
2 CONC ied at Wolfsheim, in the Palatinate. Their children were Peter, Ann,  Martin, Jacob, Henry, Dielman and Johannes. 
2 CONT
