1 NOTE Source of information on this family: "The Strassburger Family and Allied Families of Pennsylvania", Ralph Beaver Strassburger, 1922.  In the summer of 1717, Dielman and Elizabeth emigrated from Germany to Pennsylvania, arriving o
2 CONC n 10 Aug 1717 at Philadelphia, and settled in Salford Twp. [then Philadelphia Co.], where Dielman became a preacher in the Skippack and Salford Mennonite congregations. He was one of founders of the Mennonite Church at Salford in 
2 CONC 1738, and was one of a committee to arrange and supervise the translation of the "Martyr's Mirror" from the Dutch to the German language.  Dielman Kolb felt strongly about the importance of translating the "Martyr's Mirror", also 
2 CONC called "The Bloedigh Tooniel" or Bloody Theatre, an account of the Mennonite's struggles and sufferings, from its original Dutch (published in 1659) into German. After translators and a printer were located at Ephrata, Lancaster C
2 CONC o, PA, he and Henry Funk oversaw the translation and printing. It took fifteen men three years to accomplish this, finishing in 1749. The tome was about 1,500 pages and was sold for 20 shillings per copy. It was eventually publish
2 CONC ed in English in 1837, translated by I. Daniel Rupp.
