1 OCCU Farmer, Deacon, Cofounder of the Mennonite Congregation of Skippack, Valentie Hunsicker came to Philadelphia in 1717. He was a Deacon at Skippack and is buried at the Skippack Meeting House Cemetery.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Hunsicker Family History by Henry A. Hunsicker - Pub. 1911
2 CONT
2 CONT Our ancestor Valentine Hunsicker was born in the Canton Zurich, Switzerland, <nowiki>-----</nowiki>, 1700.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an orphan and came with his maternal grandfather, Valentine Klemmer, to America in 1717. Died 30 Mar 1771.
2 CONT
2 CONT After a short stay at Germantown, PA, among the Palatinate settlers, the family in company with some others pushed into the interior of the dense forests of Eastern Pennsylvania, going as far north as northern Bucks Co., into a di
2 CONC strict known as "der gross Schwamm"  (big swamp), and later called Lower Milford, which name it still retains.
2 CONT
2 CONT In common with other primitive settlers, their house was built of roughly hewn logs, plastered with clay, and their living depended on what they could extract from hasty clearings and from the game then abounding in these wilds. I
2 CONC t is needless to add that they were poor, compelled to depend mainly on axe and spade for a living. Young Valentine was at once thrown upon his own resources as a laborer, and was hired out to chop wood, and at intervals assist hi
2 CONC s grandfather at weaving, to help pay for the cost of his passage across the ocean.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1720 or 1721 he went, with his uncle Henry Klemmer, who was a stone-mason, to Van Bebber (now called Skippack) and assisted him as a laborer. He helped his uncle to builld the first Mennonite meeting-house there in 1724 (the
2 CONC  second built in America; that at Germantown was built first, in 1708). He remained in Skippack, and by industry and economy saved enough to purchase 125 acres of wild land, for which he paid five shillings per acre, to which he s
2 CONC ubsequently added 100 acres more. The same or part of the same was lately occupied by Henry B. Hunsicker of the sixth generation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philadelphia County Records show, by census taken in 1756 by Walter Johnson by order of the Provincial Government, That Valentine Hunsicker had five children, and was assessed for 225 acres of land, 100 acres being clear.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1728; the name of his first wife is unknown. By his first marriage he had two children, the eldest, a daughter, Elizabeth; she married a man named Ruth (Henry Ruth Jr.), of which family we have lost trace; and a son 
2 CONC named Samuel, who never married. In 1732 Valentine's wife died.
2 CONT
2 CONT He afterward, in 1735, married Elizabeth Kolb, daughter of Jacob Kolb. I have her ancestry traced back six generations to Holland, through the Von Sintern, Vander Mers, and the De Vossens, very distinguished families of Amsterdam.
2 CONC  Two members, at different times, were Bergomeisters (Mayors) of their native cities.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valentine Hunsicker became a deacon in the Mennonite Church and was active in building up the congregation in Skippack, though his ancestors in Switzerland were Reformed by persevering toil and industry, he had cleared fully one-h
2 CONC alf of his land in his lifetime. Had a family of seven children: five sons, Jacob, Isaac, and Henry (our grandfather), and two daughters, Sara and Catherine (two children, Valentine and Abraham, died in infancy). He died 3 Mar 177
2 CONC 1, and lies interred in the old Mennonite burying-ground in Skippack, but no stone marks his resting place. He directed, by will, that his son Henry, though still a minor at his father's death, should come in possession of the far
2 CONC m, which  he did. He in turn left it to his son (our uncle Garret). Garret left it to his son Christian, Christian to his son Garret. Garret's son Henry lately occupied the same. It belonged to the family almost 170 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                            WILL of VALENTINE HUNSICKER
2 CONT
2 CONT Be it known to all persons whom it doth concern, that I, Valentine Hunsicker of Perkioming & Skippack Township in the County of Philadelphia and province of Pennsylvania, Farmer, being at present of good health and understanding p
2 CONC raised be the Lord for the same, but at the same time considering my mortality, do therefore hereby make my last Will and Testament in Manner following to wit -- First, I recommend my soul into the Hands of Almighty God my Creator
2 CONC  and my body to the earth to be decently buried agreeable to the orders of my Executors. And concerning my worldly goods and affairs it is my will and I dispose thereof as followeth. First it is my will that all my just debts and 
2 CONC funeral expenses shall be punctually and fully paid by my executors.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, I will and give unto my dear Wife, Elizabeth Hunsicker, our matrimonial bed and double apparel for change the bedstead and Curtains, her Cloaths our Cloaths Press and that chest from her Father she has brought to me, her new
2 CONC  side saddle and bridle and so much of kitchen and house funiture as she chuseth for herself not to exceed ten pounds, and any one of my cows which she leaseth for ever.  Item it is my will and I give order to my dear Wife Elizabe
2 CONC th for her widow residence a good room and kitchen in my house and free fodder and summer pasture for her cow also free fire wood in the yard and fitted for the stove and hearth liberty of so much fruit in the orchard as she has o
2 CONC ccasion for her own use, and the fourth of the kitchen garden and so much dung as she shall want for the garden and the interest of two hundred pounds yearly and every year as long as she shall remain my widow, but if she shall ma
2 CONC rry again then only the sum of one hundred pounds Pennsylvaina money in lieu of her full third part of my real and personal estate for ever.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item it is my will and I give unto my son Henry Hunsicker his heirs and assigns forever to hold my land and plantation wheron I now live situate in Perkioming and Skippack Townships in the county and province aforesaid, containing
2 CONC  one hundred and fifteen acres together with the buildings and improvements (excepting thereout the widow residence devised to his mother) and I do value the same to him three hundred pounds Pennsylvania money only so that he have
2 CONC  his part thereof  like the other heirs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item, since each of my other sons have received a horse so likewise bequeath one of the value of fifteen pounds to my son Henry or instead thereof that money before hand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item it is my will and I give until my son Jacob Thirty pounds as his wages for the time he has lived with me after he was of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I will that my son Henry shall work for his mother and serve her obediently until he be of age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item I will and give unto my son Samuel Hunsicker beforehand because he is my eldest son one english Silver Shilling. Further is is my will that all my children one like the other (save what is above mentioned) shall inherit in th
2 CONC e following manner to wit my son Samuel one share provided that whilst he is unmarried he shall not have his hereditary share in his own hands but it shall remain in the hands of my executors who shall pay him yearly interest ther
2 CONC efore at the rate of four per cent and no more, and the children of my daughter Elizabeth one share, my son Jacob one share, my son Isaac one share, my daughter Sarah the wife of Gottshalk Gottshalk one share and my daughter Cathe
2 CONC rine the wife of Gerhard Clemens one share, my son Henry one share, but if my son Henry shall die in his minority and unmarried his hereditary share shall be devided in equal parts among his surviving brothers and sisters or the h
2 CONC eirs of them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Item it is my will and I do hereby make nominate and ordain my dear Wife, Elizabeth Hunsicker and my son Jacob Hunsicker, Executors of this my last will and testament and I do give to them jointly or to the survivor of them full p
2 CONC ower to execute and compleat everything agreeable to this my will and testament at the same time I do hereby revoke and annul all and every will and testament heretofore by me made declairing and acknowledging this only to be my l
2 CONC ast Will and Testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT IN WITNESS WHEREOF, I have hereunto set my hand and seal the Twenty-first day of March in the year of our Lord one thousand seven hundred and Sixty-eight.
2 CONT
2 CONT Valentine Hunsicker (SEAL)
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed, sealed, and by the said testator declared for and as his last Will and Testament in the presence of us as
2 CONT
2 CONT WITNESSES:                                               Isaac Kolb                           Dielman Kolb 
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
