1 NOTE 1. When MARY KNERR, widow of JOSEPH DETWEILER RITTENHOUSE, died on 12 July 1835 she left a Will which she had written on 7 March 1834. She ordered her estate to be divided into two equal shares. One share was to go to her daughter
2 CONC  SUSAN, married to SILAS LEVERING. The other share was to be invested and the interest was to be paid to her other daughter, MARY, wife of JOHN ALEZANDER. However it explicitly states this in no way is to come under the power of h
2 CONC er husband JOHN ALEXANDER. When daughter MARY died, the capital was to be divided equally among any lawful children she may have at that date. She appointed her brother JOHN KNERR and son-in-law SILAS LEVERING as Executors. 
2 CONT When they filed the return of inventory on 2 October 1835 it was valued at $2,438.57. Among her possessions were the medical books and instruments left to her by her son JOSEPH who had died in 1832. 
2 CONT This Will file is #126 Book 11 Page 497 and is held by the Register of Wills in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. 
