1 NOTE 1. JACOB ELWOOD RITTENHOUSE moved to Juniata valley about 1810, and located at Three Locks, now Lockport in Oliver Township. He removed to Granville Township, Mifflin County, where he engaged in farming and blacksmithing until his
2 CONC  removal to Lewistown. where he became proprictor of "Peacock Major's Hotel". Retiring from the business, he followed other occupations until his death, which occurred at the age of sixty-three. Born in Montgomey Co., PA, , Februa
2 CONC ry 10, 1801. (This article is from Biographical Encyclopedia of Juniata Valley, PA, dated 1897). (Conflict of date of birth of 6 and 10) When he was nine years old the family removed to Mifflin Co. After attending the district sch
2 CONC ools, he learned the trade of stone mason and bricklayer. He was widely known as a skilled mechanic. He and his brother, Samuel, constructed many of the principal buildings in Lewistown. Mr. Rittenhouse was a worthy citizen, and i
2 CONC nfluential in the community. He was a democrat, and served in township offices. Jacob wasmarried February 10, 1825, to Margaret Keys. Jacob was a member of the German Baptist church at Lockport. This report reflects Jacob died on 
2 CONC June 6. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION MR. PAUL M. RITTENHOUSE SR., State College, Pa. USA. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1860 at age 59, JACOB ELWOOD RITTENHOUSE was farming in Granville Township, Mifflin County, Pa. His wife MARGARET KEYES died in 1857 but still living with him were some of their children. REBECCA 34, MATILDA 17, JACOB 15, CLARA
2 CONC  1O, His son SAMUEL, age 25, was living on the next farm with his wife HENRIETTA and his personal goods were valued at $100. It appears JACOB did not own the property where he was farming as there is no assessment for land. His pe
2 CONC rsonal goods were assessed at $1,000. His son GEORGE was farming just down the road. Mifflin County 1860 Census Roll 1141 Book 1 Page 181.
