2 CONT William UMSTED
2 CONT
2 CONT There are many buildings within Fairmount Park, old structures that survive, often with a purpose different than from their original use. On any given journey through the natural beauty of the Wissahickon, one is never too far fro
2 CONC m the changes in history represented by the mill turned into a canoe club, for instance, or the tavern used as a police station. Whatever is known of the buildings that remain in Fairmount Park, many of their long-ago uses are not
2 CONC  as commonly remembered. Consider for instance the story of one of the eighteenth-century Rittenhouse homes on the Monoshone Creek, a feeder of the Wissahickon. Over eighty years ago, 206 Lincoln Drive was the centerpiece in a bat
2 CONC tle between a woman running a hospital and the City of Philadelphia. The story lends a lot to the story of the formation of Fairmount Park and it helps fill out a chapter in the history of one of the oldest private homes in the ar
2 CONC ea.
2 CONT
2 CONT Used as a family home by the Rittenhouses since the 1720s, 206 Lincoln and its seven acre property served as a health care facility from 1891 t 1917. Not that it was known by such a modern term one hundred years ago; rather it was
2 CONC  called the Tubercular Sanitarium Recovery Center and was owned by William Umsted, a Rittenhouse descendant (Lesley 4). The steady breezes, the view overlooking the creek, and the prevalence of sunshine on the side of the hill mad
2 CONC e it ideal for the care of tuberculosis patients who needed fresh air and sunshine as part of the turn-of-the century treatment of the disease. Indeed, the sight was called probably the healthiest as well as one of the most beauti
2 CONC ful locations in Philadelphia (City Beauty Spot). Front and back porches were added on to the house in the 1890s. They were built wide enough for large wheel chairs to fit outside to enjoy the health benefits of the site. In the l
2 CONC ater years as a hospital, small outbuildings resembling beach cabanas were built to accommodate patient needs.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/34496320/william-m-umsted Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 2 2022, 13:08:37 UTC''
