2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/6c/9d/40/ff/53444860893bdee0/53444860893bdee0_original.jpg?hash=f5795906ea58025d8d4c313e4b5f3db3d3ade7949c5f08102e8f3d9e899940aa.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL Mary  Rittenhouse  (born Reiner)
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE 1. MARY REINER, widow of JACOB DETWEILER RITTENHOUSE, wrote her Will on 1 September 1873 and actually died on 17 April 1874. she directed in her Will that all her household good be sold after her decease except those specifically 
2 CONC bequeathed as follows. To her son DAVID R. RITTENHOUSE, she left $450 plus the bed, bedding and clockfrom her bedroom. She poin=ted out she was doing this for DAVID "because of his working over age,". 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her clothing and crockery were to divided "share and share alike" among her daughters. Everything else from the sale of her belongings was to be divided equally among all her children. She appointed her sons DAVID and HENRY Execut
2 CONC ors. 
2 CONT
2 CONT MARY REINER had 6 shares in the Farmers and Merchants Bank of Manixville and 3 United States Bonds. There is no mention of real estate so I assume the property had transferred to the sons when the father died. When DAVID and HENRY
2 CONC  filed the inventory on 17 April 1875, it amounted to $4,629.26. There were many children to share this amount but no distribution record in the file. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This is Will file RW #22,508 and is held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of the children of JACOB DETWEILER RITTENHOUSE filed a petition in the Orphan's Court asking for the appointment of a guardian. They were WILLIAM, ELIZABETH and ANN and as they were minors but above the age of 14 years, they
2 CONC  filed the petition on their own behalf. These appointments of guardians were important in protecting the interests of children in their parent's estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the case of RACHEL and SAMUEL they were minors under the age of 14 years. So their mother, MARY REINER, had to file the petition for a guardian to be appointed. She recommended Abraham Isett as a suitable person. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These petitions are in File OC 14,809 and OC 15,818 in the Orphan's Court records held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown. Pa. No date is shown but it must have been after 17 April 1843.because that is the date the fa
2 CONC ther died. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventiry of JACOB's personal possessions, "goods and chattels" was taken on 23 May 1843 and filed with the Court on 25 may 1843. It totalled $3,135.20. By law at that date, the widow was allowed to retain whatever goods she c
2 CONC hose up to the value of $300. These detailed inventories are not only very interesting but give an idea of the value of goods in those days. It is true that if the appraiser kept the values low, the widow's $300 would go much furt
2 CONC her. There was no estate transfer tax back then so it didn't really matter to anybody except the heirs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For instance he had 3 horses, a bay horse which they valued at $100 but the Grey horse only at $25 and a black horse at $15. Obviously they took into consideration the condition and age of the horses. A mare and colt were valued a
2 CONC t $78. househod goods such as a corner cupboard, only at $6. The low value on spinning wheels was a surprise. 5 of them were valued only at $1,50. The appraiser had to include every nut and bolt which makes the inventory in this c
2 CONC ase 4 legal pages long. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 3 May 1843 the widow, MARY REINER, filed a notice to the Court that she renounced all her rights of administration in favour of her 3 sons, HENRY R, DAVID R. and Martin Rittenhouse, so they were appointed as Admistrators. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 21 August 1843 the widow MARY REINER and her 11 children submitted a petition to the Orphan's Court to deal with two properties JOSEPH DETWEILER RITTENHOUSE had owned. One was where they had been living, in Upper Providence Tow
2 CONC nship, Montgomery County, Pa. and the other was in Limerick Township, Also in Montgomery County. It was immediatley obvious the propety could not be divided fairly among all the heirs so it would have to be sold. The Court appoint
2 CONC ed 7 men to make the appraisal. They were John Dismant, Henry Wismer, Benjamin Bean, Charles Tyson, John Rambo, Samuel Hallman and John Thomas. The land contained 98 and three quarter acres and they appraised it at $59 an acre. So
2 CONC  the total came to $5,826.25. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of JACOB's brothers offered to buy it a the appraised amount which would save the trouble of an auction. However there was a mortgage outstanding of $1,581.29 which had to be paid off. So the net amount to go to the estate w
2 CONC as $4,244.96. of this the widow, MARY REINER, was entitled to her one third dowere right which amounted to $1,414.99. the amount going to each child was $257.27 to be paid one year from 20 November 1843. When Mary Reiner died each
2 CONC  child would receive $128.63. 
2 CONT
2 CONT All that went off fairly smoothly but when it came to the second property in Limerick Township, everything got considerably more confused. The 7 appraisers found the land contained 98 acres and 40 perches. They appraised it at $50
2 CONC  per acre. This amounted to a value of $5,109. But then it was disclosed that on 1 April 1843 JACOB RITTENHOUSE had closed a deal for this land with JAMES YERGEY. It was in writing but it hadn't been finalized when JACOB died on 1
2 CONC 7 April 1843, just over 2 weeks later. So they summoned everybody involved to a Court session. What happened there is not included in the files that have survived. 
2 CONT
2 CONT It was not until 6 October 1947 that the Court ordered the property to be sold at auction.  " After first giving due and timely public notice by hand bills and advertisements for at least four successive weeks. They did on Monday 
2 CONC the eighth of November 1847 on the premises at one o'clock in the afternoon expose the real estate in the said order more fully described to Public Auction and Outcry and sold the same to William R. Rittenhouse and Charles Tyson f
2 CONC or the price or sum of forty-five dollars per acre for a total of $4,470.". 
2 CONT
2 CONT All of the above is contained in Orphan's Court Files OC 15,549 and RW 15,268 which are held by Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pa. 
