2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/08/0b/92/54/53444860893cf441/53444860893cf441_original.jpg?hash=05868103960f0f7f2841d3a3daea72acd6979fe980013a221c008d4fdbc688b6.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL David Reiner Rittenhouse
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE 1. DAVID RITTENHOUSE was dead by the time of the 1880 census for Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania. It shows ANN JOHNSON, his widow, running the farm with the help of her children. The location was explain
2 CONC ed in the biographical notes for DAVID. The children were MARY 22, HENRY 15, DAVID 13, EMMA 12, JACOB 10 and SAMUEL 6 born less than 18332 years before his father DAVID died. Found in Montgomery County 1880 census Roll 1159 Book 1
2 CONC  Page 164. 
2 CONT
2 CONT There are very seldom any dates on the Petitions to the Court to appoint guardians for minor children. However in this case the ages of the children are mentioned so we can figure it was made in 1878/9, The petition was made by th
2 CONC eir mother ANN JOHNSON for DAVID 12, EMMA 10, JACOB 8 and SAMUEL 3 years old. As usual in these petitions they ask the Court to appoint a guardian to protect their interests. They are all minor children under the age of 14 so the 
2 CONC surviving parent has to make the petition. Orphan's Court File OC #15,100. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1900 ANN was running this very large farm and all the children were still with her. Amazingly none of them have married and yet MARY is 43, HENRY 35, DAVID 33, JACOB 30 and SAMUEL 26. Found in Montgomery county 1900 census Roll
2 CONC  1444 Book 2 Page 245. 
2 CONT In 1910 ANN was still operating the farm in Upper Providence Township. Her son JACOB was married to STELLA in 1902 so he was no longer around to help. However 4 of ANN's adult children were still living with her. But with JACOB go
2 CONC ne it became necessary to hire a farm helper and this was George Harris. It shows on the 1900 and 1910 census that ANN and David Rittenhouse had another 3 children who died either at childbirth or very young as they were not named
2 CONC  on any cnesus. Found on Montgomery County 1910 census Roll 1380 Book 1 Page 25. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ANN JOHNSON, widow of DAVID RITTENHOUSE, died Intestate on 18 April 1918. As mentioned above she still owned a very large farm as well as some investments. Two of her sons, HENRY and DAVID, were appointed as Administrators of the 
2 CONC estate. They filed an inventory on 3 July 1919, totalling $23,832.68. Of this amount $9900 covered the valuation of a farm propery ANN had purchased in 1897. It was 97 acres in LimerickTownship, Montgomery County, Pa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By this date the government had legislated Inheritance Tax. The appraisers for the government immediatley filed an appeal stating the valuation for tax purposes should have been $23,853.88 and NOT $7,950.89 as the Administrators h
2 CONC ad filed. The Orphan's Court appointed an appraiser of their own and filed a petition on 3 September 1919 with the Court, pointing out all the mistakes the Administrators had made in their appraisal. Some of this centred around th
2 CONC e property in Limerick which the Administrators had ignored in their original filing. 
2 CONT That was the beginning of a long legal battle. JACOB, one of the sons and a legatee, asked the Court to release whatever they thought fair and he would settle for that amount as his share. He wanted to buy a house and needed the m
2 CONC oney to do that. In the end the Judge of the 38th Pennyslvania Judicial District ruled the government had no right to appeal in the first place and dismissed the case. There is no final distribution detail in this file so we don't
2 CONC  know what amount each of the children received. All living children would have an equal right to share in the estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT These huge files are RW # 33,837 and OC #32,231. They are both held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT DAVID RITTENHOUSE was farming in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania. in 1850. His farm was appraised at $5.000. Living with him on the farm were his brothers MARTIN 39, JACOB 31 SAMUEL 18, sister RACHEL 20 
2 CONC and their widowed mother MARY REINER. Found in Montgomery County 1850 census Roll 799 Book 1 Page 292. 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1870 DAVID was still farming on the same land but it was much enlarged and improved. It was located south of Trappe and on the border of Upper Providence and Limerick Townships. It was valued at $10,000 and his personal goods a
2 CONC t $2,500. But about 1856 he married ANN JOHNSON who was only 24 and DAVID was 49. Not so unusual for a second marriage but rather unusual at the time for a first marriage. The 6 children they had since their marriage were MARY 12,
2 CONC  ANN 8, HENRY 5, DAVID 3, EMMA 2 and JACOB only 2 months old. DAVID'S widowed mother MARY REINER, age 85 was also living with them. Shown in Montgomeery County 1870 census Roll 1379 Book 1 Page 375. 
2 CONT
2 CONT DAVID RITTENHOUSE died on 6 October 1876. His widow ANN JOHNSON filed a petition with the Orphan's Court on behalf of her 4 minor children who were under the age of 14 years, to have a guardian appointed to protect their interest 
2 CONC in any estate left by the deceased father. When children were under the age of 14 a parent had to petition on their behalf whereas if they were over 14 they petitioned on their own. The children were DAVID 12, EMMA 10, JACOB 5 and
2 CONC  SAMUEL 8. This petition is file # 0C 15,100 in the Orphan's Court and is held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Court appointed the widow, ANN JOHNSON, and DAVID's brother, HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE, Executors of the deceased DAVID's estate. By law the widow could choose up to $300 value of goods before the estate was settled. She chose 
2 CONC some goods but elected to take $248,90 in cash on 2 November 1876. This is the first time I have come across the Court allowing the widow to take part cash as her legal right. She usually had to wait until the estate is settled to
2 CONC  see whether there is any cash left after the epenses and debts are paid. 
2 CONT In the the meantime HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE died so a new Executor had to be appointed which was HENRY KRATZ who was the Executor appointed by HENRY REINER RITTENHOUSE in his will. When they took the inventory it totalled $1,572.
2 CONC 28. However the debts and expenses totalled $1,824.02 so the estate was declared insolvent. That meant there was no money left for the widow and children so maybe ANN JOHNSON was clever in taking some cash as part of her $300 wido
2 CONC w exemption. This is all found in Orphan's Court File OC 15,099 and Will File RW 15,063 and they are held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pennsylvania. 
