1 NOTE 1. When WILLIAM RITTENHOUSE's father JACOB died, he and his sisters ELIZABETH and ANN, petitioned the Court to have a Guardian appointed to protect their interest in the estate. As they were all minors, but over the age of 14 year
2 CONC s, they could petition on their own behalf. This petition is file RW 14952 and held by the Montgomery County Archives in Norristown, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM RITTENHOUSE. age 27, was farming in Upper Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania in 1850. His real estate was valued at $1,300 but it is not clear whether he actually owned this farm. With him were his wife E
2 CONC LIZABETH LINDERMAN, age 23, and their recently born daughter EMMA. Shown in Montgomery County 1850 census Roll 799 Book 1 Page 28
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1870 WILLIAM RITTENHOUSE was a hotel keeper at the Eaglesville Hotel in Eaglesville, Lower Providence Township, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania. The 1871 Montgomery County Historical Atlas shows the location of the hotel on the
2 CONC  west side of Ridge Pike right in Eaglesville.The census shows his real estate valued at $7,000 and personal goods at $3,000. By the 1877 map WILLIAM has departed and J. C. Laver was shown as running the hotel. However in 1870 his
2 CONC  wife ELIZABETH LINDERMAN and 3 of their children were living there. The children were EMMA 20, ANNIE 18 and LIZZIE 12. Found in Montgomery County 1870 census Roll 1377 Book 1 Page 552 
2 CONT
2 CONT By 1880 WILLIAM was still living somewhere in Lower Providence Township but he was by then a Live Stock Dealer. All of the children have departed from home but his wife ELIZABETH LINDERMAN is with him. Found in Montgomery County 1
2 CONC 880 cenus Roll 1158 Book1 Page 284. 
