2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/75085659/john-m-heckler Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 15 2022, 10:57:52 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT Obit for John Heckler 30 Apr 1937
2 CONT
2 CONT Many Friends Attend Rites Jno. M. Heckler
2 CONT Former County Assessor is Laid to Rest Here Thursday--Rev. George Hirst of St. James' Conducts Impressive Services at the Creel Chapel
2 CONT
2 CONT Many friends gathered at the Creel chapel Thursday afternoon to pay their last tributes of love and respect to the memory of John M. Heckler, former county assessor and well known bank employee who died at his home here Monday eve
2 CONC ning following several years of failing health.
2 CONT Services at the chapel were conducted by the Rev. George Hirst, long time friend and associate of Mr. Heckler, and following these services a large number followed the casket to the Lewistown cemetery where burial was made with th
2 CONC e impressive rites of the Masonic lodge being carried out by officers and members of Lewistown lodge No. 37, A.F.&A.M., all of whom acted as honorary pallbearers.
2 CONT The active pallbearers, divided into two groups included David Bolton, George Fraser, J.A. Elliott, C.A. McDonald, C.L. Covell, Oscar Mueller, Luther Warden, William Dissly, E.L. Thielke, George Boettcher, Dr. C.W. Cox and Frank C
2 CONC riswell.
2 CONT In his funeral address, Mr. Hirst said:
2 CONT "If it were customary for me to take a text on an occasion like this, my text would be, "And Enoch walked with God, and he was not, for God took him." This text is found in Gen.5:24. But if you refer to Hebrews 11:4, "By faith Eno
2 CONC ch was translated that he could not see death; and was not found, because God had translated him; for before his translation, he had this testimony that he pleased God." That might be said of John M. Heckler.
2 CONT "From time to time a clergyman is called upon for his services at the time of death, and in some cases the person who has died is better or not, all known to the clergyman. You don't need me to tell you anything more about that re
2 CONC lationship. It cannot possibly be personal. The present case does not fall in that class. Very far from it. He and his wife loved music. More than twenty years ago John M.Heckler and his good wife sang in the choir at the church w
2 CONC here I enjoy the privilege of serving, for a full year. I grew to know them well, and through the succeeding years on many occasions our paths have met.
2 CONT "When Mr. Heckler became our county assessor, I had reason to speak to him about a small piece of property which perhaps had not been properly assessed. He made a journey in person to the place and corrected the matter, making him
2 CONC self responsible to me for the entire matter.
2 CONT "That was emblematic of the man. Sincerity and honesty were deeply ingrained in the character of John M.Heckler.
2 CONT "He was a man possessed of radiant faith. His belief shone in his face. He made no apologies for his religious relationships, for as we said in the beginning, 'He walked with God, and God took him.'
2 CONT "If you would read as I have read, the testimony of young men whose lives were enriched by him, as he taught them in Sunday school, I think you would be stirred. There are few men teaching in our Sunday schools. I thank God for th
2 CONC e men who give themselves in unselfish service for our many organizations. You will not blame me if I covet men who are willing to bring to bear the wight of their own characters upon our youth in religious contacts. John Heckler 
2 CONC did this in a manly, straightforward way.
2 CONT "In this day and age, we hear and read and observe how loosely in many cases, the marriage tie is held. It was always a benediction to be in his home. It was a living example of that motto, "There is beauty all around when there i
2 CONC s love at home."
2 CONT "You men and women knew him in his banking activities, and the charm of his smile made it a pleasure to transact business with him.
2 CONT "All these things were to John Heckler as stepping stones to higher things. It is more that a human satisfaction to realize that he has entered upon the possession of those good things which pass our understanding.
2 CONT "A staunch believer in Jesus Christ, without any hesitation or equivocation whatever. Because he believed, he taught others by his life and by his words that story old but never new. Man's necessity was God's opportunity. "You nee
2 CONC d a Saviour, I will send you one. He came after what to human beings was a long time of waiting. 'In the fulness of time He came.' That means in the providence of God, when He was ready for it.
2 CONT "A baby, shepherds came; wise men, to worship him, born King of the Jews. Lived patently--came out quietly without any blare of trumpets to do His work. Children loved Him. He healed the sick, raised the dead, cleansed the lepers.
2 CONC  He gave us an example that we should follow His steps.
2 CONT "Twelve men--one dead by his own hand. Where were they? In your upper room frightened--lest they too must suffer. But when they were convinced that He was risen, they no longer hid. Will you copy them in relation to your risen Lor
2 CONC d. John Heckler did.
2 CONT "John M. Heckler was born near Philadelphia, Pa., Aug. 18, 1869. He received his early training at Mt. Carroll and Mt. Morris colleges in Illinois, and soon after completing his schooling was married to Miss Hattie Phillips. Follo
2 CONC wing the wedding, they moved to St. Joseph, Mo. where he started his long banking career.
2 CONT "He came first to Stanford in 1912, then moved to Roy, and to Lewistown in 1015, when he was employed in the Empire Bank & Trust Co., and also in the old Bank of Fergus.
2 CONT "He was a member of the Masonic order since he was 21 years of age, and he was a faithful Mason, serving as treasurer of Lewistown lodge No.37 for several years.
2 CONT "Always a lover of music, he made his own contribution on many occasions.
2 CONT "Bobby Burns says, "Princes and lords are but the breath of kings. An honest man's the noblest work of God.'
2 CONT "John Heckler answers to that description. Twice on two days previous to his death, he said to his wife, 'I am going home.'"
