1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A PIONEER PASSES AWAY MRS. ELIZABETH GOTWALS EXPIRES AFTER LONG ILLNESS SAD FUNERAL HELD IN HYATTVILLE Death Of Pioneer Of The West And One Of The Earliest Settlers In The Basin. Mrs. Elizabeth Gotwals, who had bee
2 CONC n taken to her home on Medicine Lodge from the Basin hospital last Monday, expired Wednesday night. Mrs. Gotwals for the past two years had been suffering and for her ailment no hopes were entertained by leading physicians In the 
2 CONC east. Mrs. [sic] Elizabeth Harris was born in Independence, Missouri, and at the time of her demise was 61 years of age. She was the fourth woman to come to Deadwood, S. D., having accompanied her parents there in 1876. In the fol
2 CONC lowing year she was married to Lorenzo D. Waln, who died in Idaho about fifteen years ago. To this union three sons were born, who survive her, Claude, Ren and Elmer, all residents on Medicine Lodge. She is also survived by two br
2 CONC others, J. H. Harris of Lovell and D. L. Harris of Jerome, Idaho. Deceased was united in marriage with Abraham Gotwals of Hyattville about seven years ago and he passed beyond about three years since. Mrs. Gotwals settled on Medic
2 CONC ine Lodge creek in 1883 and was pioneer of Big Horn county. She was a lady of high character and refinement and during her long residence in this country had endeared herself to practically all of the old settlers. She was a membe
2 CONC r of the Rebekah lodge of Midvale, Idaho, and took great interest in the work of that society until her health failed.
