1 NAME Henry Alderfer /Hunsicker/
2 GIVN Henry Alderfer
2 SURN Hunsicker
2 _MARNM Hunsicker
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 10 NOV 1825
2 ADDR
3 CITY Upper Providence
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 9 MAY 1874
2 PLAC Trinity UCC Cemetery, Collegeville, Montgomery, Pa
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 2 JUN 1916
2 ADDR
3 CITY Upper Providence
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/20998524/henry-alderfer-hunsicker
2 CONT likeness and bio. at https://gameo.org/index.php?title=Hunsicker_Group
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was the son of Abraham Detweiler Hunsicker and Elizabeth Shoemaker Alderfer. He married Mary S. Weinberger on 23 Aug 1849. Henry and Mary had five children, Clement, Joseph, Abraham, Flora and H. Alvin. Henry married secondl
2 CONC y Anna K. Gotwals on 11 May 1876. Henry and Anna had two children, Mary Ann and Edna. Henry was the compiler of the Hunsicker Family History of 1911. ....................................... Henry Alderfer Hunsicker: His father was
2 CONC  a farmer, and as a farmer's boy the subject of this sketch was brought up. He was instructed in the duties of farm life in the period when the sickle, the sythe, and the grain cradle were the reaping implements; when the flair an
2 CONC d horse-treading were processes of threshing out the grain; when the flax was raised and in all its processes of manufacture mainly supplied what cotton does now; when sheep were raised on the farm and furnished the wool which was
2 CONC  spun, woven, and made into the family clothing; when what wood was the fuel and coal scarcely thought of; when tallow candles and oil lamps furnished the light; when the tinder-box, with ist burned rags, flint and steel, was depe
2 CONC nded on to start the morning fire. These and other slow, primitive drawbacks, compared with modern improved mechanical appliances and labor-saving machinery, were trials to contend with, but they were not without benefit in streng
2 CONC thening and developing the body and adding tone and vigor to mind and character as well. The great inventions and their adaptation to the needs of humanity, the real glories of the nineteenth century, far surpass all the discoveri
2 CONC es of the of the eighteen previous centuries. These have been brought about during his lifetime.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poor schools and incompetent teachers (then called school-masters) and winter sessions of three months afforded very limited opportunity for learning. There was no system or classification or uniform set of text-books. To a boy fo
2 CONC nd of books and study, it may be well imagined, there was a gloomy outlook. The father, noting the tendency of the boy'sfondness for study, sent him at the age of ten years, to Henry Prizer's boarding school at Trappe. Mr. Prizer 
2 CONC died in 1838, and was succeeded by Rev. Henry S. Rodenbaugh. He continued under Mr. Rodenbaugh's instruction until 1845. Later he became a pupil of Rev. Samuel Aaron at Norristown, PA.
2 CONT
2 CONT By diligence and close application he made fair progress. In the fall of 1843 he was induced to take charge of a country subscription school in Lower Salford, Montg. Co. He was required to teach German along with the usual English
2 CONC  branches.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus for five years alternately going to school and teaching, he was induced by his father, in the fall of 1848, to take charge of Freeland Seminary, a boarding school for young men and boys which his father founded the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Assisted by a corps of able instructors, all the common branches, together with the higher mathematics, the sciences, and ancient and modern languages, were taught. The school grew in numbers and reputation, and had among its pupi
2 CONC ls Wayne MacVeagh (afterwards Attorney-General under President Garfield), his brother Franklin Mac Veagh (the present Secretar of the Treasurey under President taft), John R. Brooke, Major General of the Army of the United States,
2 CONC  and many other men of note.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under the blessing of Providence the school proved to be eminently prosperous, so that at the close of his administration in 1865 nearly 3600 young men and boys had been students under him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After seventeen years of devoted labor as principal of Freeland Seminary, he leased the school to Prof. A. H. Fetterolf (now Dr. Fetterolf, ex-Pressident of Girard College), who also had been a pupil of Freeland Seminary, and beca
2 CONC me professor of mathematics, and vice-principal of the school.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1869 he sold Freeland Seminary to a corporation headed by Dr. Bomberger of Philadelphia, who obtained a charter and opened Ursinus College.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the ownership by his father and his sons of a tract of fourteen thousand acres of timber lands purchased in 1850 in Potter and Cameron Counties, he embarked in the lumber business in the autumn of 1865, remaining engaged i
2 CONC n the same more or less up to the present time.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was called to the ministry in 1850 by the liberal wing of the Mennonite Church to assist in his father's bishopric. This he did in conjunction with his duties as principal of the school. But by reason of dissensions arising amo
2 CONC ng the members of the churches from different views held in regard to matters of discipline and church government, he left the Mennonite Church when his father did, and joined him in the enterprise of founding the Trinity Christia
2 CONC n Church at College, intended to be an independent, non-denominational church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry eventually became a Presbyterian (see https://gameo.org/index.php?title=Hunsicker_Group)
