1 NAME Naugle /Kephart/
2 GIVN Naugle
2 SURN Kephart
2 _MARNM Kephart
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Cottage Hill
3 STAE Iowa
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1 FEB 1798
2 ADDR
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 MAY 1884
2 ADDR
3 CITY Concord Twnsp.
3 STAE Iowa
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The aaccompanying typed page is a copy of a deed to Naugle Kephart for land purchased in Dubuque County, Iowa, in 1839. Milton Kephart, the first of his children to be born in Iowa, was born in December of 1839. Na
2 CONC ugle was probably born in Huntingdon Co. Pennsylvania, the son of Henry and Elizabeth Kephart. He probably had an older brother Conrad Kephart, and two younger brothers, Essington Kephart and Henry G. Kephart. Possibly there was a
2 CONC nother brother Jacob Kephart, but that is less clear. This Information is based on a brief biography of Essington Kephart, pages 1026 & 1027, History of Venango County, Pennsylvania (Library of Congress) and a brief account of the
2 CONC  sons of Henry G. Kephart pages 536, 537 History of Butler and Bremer Counties, Iowa,to be found in the Genealogical Roms of the Oregon State Library, Salem Oreegon.
2 CONT
2 CONT These accounts are augmented and substantiated by land records of Huntingdon and Venango, Pennsylvania, and of Dubuque County, Iowa. Naugle probably married in Huntingdon County, Pa., about 1821. Naugle and Conrad received a deed 
2 CONC of land from Henry and Elizabeth Kephart in 1823. They probably lived in Morris Township, Huntingdon County though they may also have been in West and Franklin Townships of that county. U.S. Census for 1830 shows Naugle and his fa
2 CONC mily living in French Creek Township, Venango County, near the town of Franklin, Pennsylvania. In October 1835 Naugle and Susan sold land in Venango County.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1856 state census for Iowa shows that Naugle and his family moved to Iowa in 1837. In July 1839 he purchased land and household goods [in Concord Township, Dubuque County, Iowa, 1 mi east of the city of Holy Cross] as this deed in
2 CONC dicates. Conrad Kephart and family came to Iowa in 1850. Henry G. Kephart and family moved from Pennsylvania to Iowa in 1848. Essington remained in Pennsylvania. A Jacob Kephart (who had a son named Conrad) moved from Pennsylvania
2 CONC  to Dubuqe County, near the others. But there is less evidence of his relationship than in the case of the other four.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naugle and Susan appear in cencus records in Concord Township,  Dubuque County, Iowa in 1850, 1856, 1870, and 1880,  Thus they both died after 1880 [and were buried at Cottage Hill Methodist Cemetery near Holy Cross, Iowa].
2 CONT
2 CONT Lewis H and Esther P. Austin       DEcember 1, 1978.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4223 N.E. Hazelfern Place
