2 CONT The children of Jacob and Nanny Wismer, as near in the order of birth as could be placed were: ---Jacob, Mary, Elizabeth, Joseph, Henry, Mark, Daniel, John, Christian, Nanny, Abraham.
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Jacob Wismer,* bn. in Bucks Co., Pa., about 1721. Mrd. Margaret ---. The dates of death of himself and wife are not known. He received his portion of his father's estate in 1783. Lived in Plumstead Twp. at what is known as the
2 CONC  burnt mill, where he followed the occupation of miller. Children: Abraham, Isaac, Anna, Mary, Elizabeth, Jacob, Mary, Barbara, Hannah, Margaret.
2 CONT
2 CONT III. Elizabeth Wismer, bn. Sept. 1, 1753; died Sept. 9, 1837. Mrd. Joseph Nash,—. He was bn. Sept. 30, 1753; died May 31, 1830. Farmer and weaver. Lived in Tinicum Twp. Members of the Menn. ch. of which he was a deacon for many ye
2 CONC ars. Children: Mary, Abraham, Jacob, Henry, Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT IV. Jacob Nash, bn. Jan. 25, 1781; died Nov. 25, 1851. Mrd. Elizabeth Myers, —. Mason and farmer. Lived in Tinicum Twp. Menn. Children: Samuel, Susan, Elizabeth, Joseph, William, Henry, Tobias, Levi, Mary, Eli, Anna, Jacob.
2 CONT
2 CONT V. Samuel Nash, bn. Jan. 10, 1810. Mrd. Barbara Wismer, Mar. 27, 1832. P. O., Doylestown, Pa. Mr. Nash followed farming for some years. He was also a well known auctioneer and general business agent. Some years ago he retired from
2 CONC  active work, and moved to Doylestown, Pa., where he still resides. Children: Sophia, Susanna, Reuben, Levi, Samuel, Huldah, Amanda, Deborah, Marietta, Lizzie.
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. Elizabeth Nash, bn. in Bucks Co., Pa., Apr. 20, 1854. Mrd. George W. Mclntosh, of Doylestown, Pa., Apr. 22, 1880. Mr. Mclntosh learned the printing trade in the office of the Bucks County Intelligencer, where for several years
2 CONC  he served as pressman, and job printer, and was subsequently appointed foreman of the establishment. In 1879 he removed to Illinois and bought one-half interest in the Republican, published at Watseka, Iroquois Co. After a short 
2 CONC residence there he sold his interest in the Republican, and returned to Doylestown, Pa., and resumed work again in the Intelligencer office in his old position as foreman. When the daily edition of the Intelligencer was started in
2 CONC  1886, he was appointed business manager, but for the past three years he has served as local editor of the daily and weekly Intelligencer. Mr. Mclntosh, Presbyterian, Mrs. Mclntosh, Baptist. One child:
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. Florence Nash Mclntosh, bn. Nov. 4, 1887.
