2 CONT VI. Joseph L. Wismer, bn. May 21, 1855. In Jan. 1879 went to Canada, remained two years. After visiting his parents in Bucks Co., in Dec. 1880, he returned to Canada and remained three years. In March 1886 he took a trip to variou
2 CONC s parts of the West, visiting Sterling, Ill., Los Angeles, Cal., and many towns and villages in California, among them San Francisco. From Los Angeles he took the largest steamer on the Pacific Ocean for San Francisco, where he re
2 CONC mained some days, then took train for Sterling, Ill., stopping at Sacramento, Salt Lake City, Utah, which he represents as the cleanest city he ever was in, and where he remained a day and a night. From thence he went to Denver, C
2 CONC olo., and on through Iowa to Sterling, Ill., remaining until Oct. 10, when, in company with Albert Myers, of Hilltown, Pa., he went to Chicago and thence to Elkhart, Ind., where he parted company with Mr. Myers, then went to Buffa
2 CONC lo, and arrived in Canada, Oct. 23, 1886. Mrd. Anna H., daughter of Rev. John F. Rittenhouse, of Jordan, Ont, Jan. 23, 1889. P. O., Gardenville, Pa. Farmer. Menn's. Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. Ada Wismer, bn. May 29, 1890.
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. Paul Wismer, bn. Nov. 2, 1892.
