1 NOTE 1. According to L.J. Burkholder in "A Brief History of Mennonites in Ontario", Joseph M. Wismer was ordained to the ministry of the Mennonite Church on Nov. 1, 1885, by Bishop Gayman of Cayuga during one of those periods when the 
2 CONC church at the "Twenty" was torn asunder by strife. Formal peace was again restored before the official break in 1889. Joseph sympathized with the Gayman party. 
2 CONT From the Canadian Fretz Descendants Newsletter - Volume 4-Issue 1, Number 7, pg.8: 
2 CONT
2 CONT " Joseph was ordained to the ministry in the Moyer Mennonite Church, Vineland, on November 1, 1885. During 1889 a group who wanted to remain more conservative separated from the Moyer congregation, and built their church building 
2 CONC on Martin Road, beside the north-west corner of the old 'Mennonite Burying Ground' stone wall. Minister Joseph and Mary Wismer went with minister Abraham F. Rittenhouse and thirty-eight others to this more conservative division. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ... After several years the membership in this congregation dwindled, until Joseph Wismer was listed in the last six members. Caretaker A.D. Moyer relates how Joseph Wismer, last pastor of this church, used to carry on services so
2 CONC metimes by himself, singing and praying. 
2 CONT Joseph Wismer died in 1927, which caused this church to close. Mary died October 28, 1911, three years before Moses F. Rittenhouse purchased the adjoining land for a community cemetery." 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE of INFORMATION 
2 CONT MRS. PATRICIA HIGH, Jordan Station, Ontario, Canada
1 CHAN
2 DATE 14 MAR 2021
3 TIME 08:59:56
