1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Brief History of Jacob Wismer and a Complete Genealogical Family Register With Biographies of His Descendants from the Earliest Available Records to the Present Time By A J Fretz, Eli Wismer 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=nwBgAAAAMAAJ 
2 CONT
2 CONT Page xiii, 7, 139, 144, 145, 179, 182
2 CONT
2 CONT I. Jacob Wismer, (...) was born in Germany about 1684, and died in Bedminster Township, Bucks Co., Pa., Feb. 4, 1787 in his one hundred and third year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT (...) 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Jacob and Nanny Wismer, as near in the order of birth as could be placed were: ---Jacob, Mary, Elizabeth, Joseph, Henry, Mark, Daniel, John, Christian, Nanny, Abraham.
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Henry Wismer, bn. in Bucks Co., Pa., about 1730; died ---. Mrd. Barbara Lederach in 1754. The children of Henry and Barbara Wismer are as follows: Jacob, Abraham, Annie, Maria, Henry, Joseph, Christian, Esther, John, Elizabeth
2 CONC , Samuel, Barbara.
2 CONT
2 CONT III. Abraham Wismer, bn. in Bucks Co., Nov. 5, 1756; died in 1844, aged 87 y. 3 m. 27 d. Mrd. Veronica Myers, —. She was bn. Apr. 3, 1757; died about 1818. Mrd second wife Mary, a widow of Detweiler (maiden name Freed). Farmer. Me
2 CONC nn. ch. Children: Henry, Samuel, Isaac, Barbara, Catharine, Veronica, Abraham, Elizabeth, Esther.
2 CONT
2 CONT IV. Esther Wismer, bn. Feb. 8, 1797; died Feb. 5, 1877. Mrd. Jacob Bergey, July 15, 1814. Children: Abraham, Susan, Jacob, John, Anna.
2 CONT
2 CONT V. Anna Bergey, bn. July 11, 1830. Mrd. William Garges, —. Farmer. Children: Amanda, Hettie, Abraham, John, Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT VI. Amanda Garges, bn. —. Mrd. Christian K. Meyer, —. P. O., Plumsteadville. Pa. Carpenter and undertaker. Menn's. One child:
2 CONT
2 CONT VII. Edwin G. Meyer, bn. in 1881.
2 CONT MOYER. ­ On Jan. 23d, 1895, in Plumsteadville, Bucks Co., Pa., of pneumonia, sister Amanda Moyer, maiden name Garges, wife of Bro. Christian K. Moyer, aged 43 y., 1 m., 16 d. Buried at Deep Run on Monday Jan. 28th. Funeral service
2 CONC s conducted at the house by Pre. Sam'l Godshalk in the English language; at the meeting house by Pre. Henry Rosenberger and Pre. Isaac Rickert. The 103 Psalm was selected by the deceased sister as her refuge while yet apparently i
2 CONC n health. Her calling away was so sudden, she being sick but a few days, the bereaved husband wished this above Psalm read at her funeral occasion. Appropriate words were spoken by both ministers to console the many mourners who h
2 CONC ad assembled from far and near, to take the last look upon her earthly tabernacle; her spirit had gone hence. ­COR
