0 @N6000000000141946740@ NOTE From "The Early Life of the Pennsylvania Germans," A. Monroe Aurand, Jr., The Aurand Press, Lancaster, PA.
1 CONT
1 CONT People from Palatinate make up much of the Pennsylvania German community.  The inhabitants of Palatinate were decended from a group of German tribes called the Rheinfranken, with a mixture of Alemanni.  The people of Palatinate we
1 CONC re good farmers, witty, industrious, and intelligent, according to the travelers through the waterways of the Palatinate.  However, from 1635-1636, there was great suffering and misery, because of wars and famine.  Cannabalism and
1 CONC  the eating of grass and roots may have resulted.  Wars ended in a compromise at Westphalia in 1649; this settled some of the conflicts between the Catholic, Lutheran, and Reformed Churches.  The Palatinate continued to be desolat
1 CONC e for a while, but eventually the people returned to their farms with more luck and church membership increased.  Under Karl Ludwig, the Mennonites, who had been oppressed before, were allowed to worship, which further helped pros
1 CONC perity.
1 CONT 1674-1675: War between France and Holland; destruction in Palatinate
1 CONT 1685-1689: Civil and religious wars, especially between the Lutherans and the Reformed (this pleased the Catholics).  Very unpleasant war.
1 CONT 1697: Peace treaty at Ryswick.  The State/Catholic Church took most of the Protestant Churches' property.  Protestant were tolerated, but had to "bend the knee at the passing of the Host" and to give up rights to church property.
1 CONT With this treaty, Huguenots, Walloons, and Swiss Mennonites were no longer allowed in the Palatinate.  Groups went to Prussia, Holland, America, and Switzerland (Switzerland, though spared the ravages of war, was ruled by an arist
1 CONC ocracy that created "menial services, high taxes and other complaints," so people were still looking for a better placed that allowd them to worship as they pleased).
1 CONT Eventually the yokes of slavery and religious persecution led Mennonites and other religious minorities to move to Lancaster County, PA (among other areas).  This mass exodus caused economic and other problems and may have helped 
1 CONC cause the French Revolution.
1 CONT The name Detweiler may be from the place Detweiler, a village in Canton Zurich.
1 CONT
1 CONT Imported from Disk 6, #1076:
1 CONT
1 CONT CHRONOLOGY OF LIFE HISTORY
1 CONT Apr 8,1724
1 CONT Hans purchases 102 acres in Bebbers Township, Philadelphia County, from Matthias Van Bebber for 25 pounds. He established the family homestead and lived on this farm until he died in 1761.
1 CONT REF: Montgomery Co. Deed Book 11:238.
1 CONT Sep 1725
1 CONT Hans, with thirty-three others signed a petition which requested the Court of Quarter Sessions of Philadelphia to "have the township regularly lay'd out by a surveyor"
1 CONT REF: Pennsylvania Magazine of History and Biography Vol.31 pp. 10,11.
1 CONT May 10, 1728
1 CONT Hans, with many others, signed a petition addressed to Governor Patrick Gordon, requesting relief from attacks by indians "about Falkners Swamp and New Coshahopin" 
1 CONT REF: History of The Mennonites of The Franconia Conference, p.407, J. C. Wenger, Pennsylvania Archives 1727 p. 213.
1 CONT 1730
1 CONT Hans is listed among the first settlers of  Philadelphia county, who were naturalized. 
1 CONT REF: Pennsylvania Archives, 2nd Series, Vol. 7, p. 127 - 129. He is also listed in Section II of the Appendix p.434 in Rupp's Index to the 30,000 Names of Immigrants in Penna. 1727-1776, published in 1975 by the Genealogical Publi
1 CONC shing Co. Inc. Baltimore, Md.
1 CONT Apr 23, 1733
1 CONT Record of Public Vendue, Sulfut Estate of Lawrence Bingham Articles Sold & Purchasers
1 CONT A Bebbell            Johannes Tettweiler    1-7-6
1 CONT A Scantlin           Do       Do                 0-12-0
1 CONT A Sow                 Do       Do                 0-11-0
1 CONT Ref: Perkiomen region Vol.12 p.20,21
1 CONT Was Johannes, actually Hans? Hans son, Johannes, would have been only 11 years old at this time. Or was there another Detweiler family?
1 CONT Nov 22,1735
1 CONT Hans received a Patent from Edward Shippen for 336 acres located in Bedminster Twp., Bucks County. 
1 CONT REF: Bucks County Rent, Roll #7, the entry reads " 5:8 1742 6 [paid] in full one Pound"
1 CONT Sep 3, 1737
1 CONT Warrant to Hans of Bucks County requests that the Proprietaries grant him to take up a vacancy of about 40 acres of land situate near the Deep Run and adjoining Edward Shippen and Detwillar's other land in Bucks County.
1 CONT [Warrant 1737, No. 14].
1 CONT Apr 16, 1739
1 CONT By virtue of the above warrant, Surveyed to Hans Dattwillar the land, 40 acres in Warrant, containing 172 acres plus 6% allowance for roads. Surveyed by Nicholas Schull and adjacent to William Allen, Matthew Hughes and Joseph Kirk
1 CONC bride and Heirs of Richard Hill, and also adjacent other land of Hans Dattwillar.
1 CONT Sep 8, 1740
1 CONT Hans received a Patent for 172 acres at 12 per acre, 
1 CONT REF: Bucks County Rent, Roll #8, Patent Book A9 p. 209.
1 CONT Mar 2, 1742
1 CONT Hans sells his son John the North-Eastern half of his 336 acre tract in Bedminster. REF: Bucks County DB 11:357.
1 CONT Sep 16, 1743
1 CONT Hans offers for sale "two properties in Bedminster Township, Bucks Co. on Deep Run."  
1 CONT REF: page 1 of Advertisements in "German Newspapers Published in Philadelphia & Germantown 1743 - 1800" compiled by Edward Hocker.
1 CONT Oct 8, 1747
1 CONT Hans sells his 172 acre and his 178 acre tract, remaining half of his 336 acre tract, to Martin Gretter.
1 CONT 1750 and 1757
1 CONT In the Alms Book of the Lower Skippack Mennonite congregation, Hans signed as an auditor twice. This occurred between 1750 and 1757. This book contains a record of alms monies received from 1738 to 1954 and is preserved in the  Ea
1 CONC stern Penna. Mennonite Historical Archives.
1 CONT 1756
1 CONT Census of Skippack lists three of Hans sons:
1 CONT Joseph Detweiler 4 children 180 Acres 70 Cleared
1 CONT Henry  Detweiler 1 child    100 Acres 60 Cleared
1 CONT John   Detweiler 8 children
1 CONT REF:"History of Skippack" by James Y. Heckler, Heckler files at the Montgomery Co. Historical Society.
1 CONT
1 CONT Burial date and location not known. Hans probably was buried in the Mennonite Society Cemetery in Skippack. 
