0 @N6000000000587297128@ NOTE
1 CONT History: Family:  The KOLB (KULP) Family: Philadelphia and Montgomery Counties, PA
1 CONT
1 CONT Contributed for use in USGenWeb Archives by Thera.  tsh@harborside.com
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is from The Strassburger Family and Allied Families of Pennsylvania,
1 CONT by Ralph Beaver Strassburger, 1922,  pp. 391-413.
1 CONT
1 CONT THE KOLP (KULP) FAMILY
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb of Wolfsheim in Baden, Germany, was born about 1648 and died in 
1 CONT 1712, aged 64 years.  He is buried at Manheim in Germany, where he resided.
1 CONT His wife, Agnes, was a daughter of Peter Schumacher, who came to Germantown
1 CONT in 1685, and died in 1707, aged 85 years.  Agnes died in Germany in 1705, aged
1 CONT 53 years, and is buried at Wolfsheim.  Peter Schumacher had five daughtes, three 
1 CONT of whom accompanied him to this country.  The fourth daughter married Reynier
1 CONT Herman Van Burkalow and came to Germantown, but later settled in Cecil County,
1 CONT Maryland.  Dielman Kolb passed away in his 65th year, five years after his wife
1 CONT died, leaving to survive him six children:  Peter, Ann, Martin, Jacob, Henry 
1 CONT and Dielman.
1 CONT
1 CONT [Note from contributor:  See below* where six sons are referenced, making seven
1 CONT children.  Actually, I believe there were a total of eight children, seven of
1 CONT them sons.  The two additional are, Johannes, b. 19 May 1683, who accompanied
1 CONT his brothers to America in 1707, and Arnold, b. 1688.]
1 CONT
1 CONT Of these, Martin, Jacob and Henry Kolb came to Pennsylvania in 1707, no doubt
1 CONT influenced to take this step by the grandfather, Peter Schumacher, who, however,
1 CONT died about the time of their arrival.  Ten years later they were followed by
1 CONT the youngest brother, Dielman.  The eldest brother, Peter, who was a Mennonite
1 CONT minister, lived and died in Manheim, Germany, and the only sister, Ann, married
1 CONT Balthaser Kolb, probably a relative, and they also remained in Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb, Sr., and his wife must have been people of means and position in
1 CONT the old country, for four of their *six sons were ministers of the Mennonite
1 CONT faith, Dielman, the youngest, being a man of exceptional education in that day.
1 CONT
1 CONT Children of Dielman Kolb and Agnes Schumacher:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.  Peter Kolb, born 1671, died 1727, aged 56 years, 8 months; buried at Manheim
1 CONT in the Palatinate.  He was a Mennonite minister.
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.  Ann Kolb, b. 1676, d. 26 Feb 1738 at Wolfsheim; married Balthasar Kolb. 
1 CONT They never came to America.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.  Martin Kolb, born 1680; came to Pennsylvana and settled in Germantown in
1 CONT 1707; married Mary Magdalena, daughter of Isaac Van Sintern and his wife Neeltje
1 CONT Claasen.  They moved to Skippack, then Philadelphia (now Montgomery) County, in
1 CONT 1709, where he was ordained a minister of the Mennonite faith, and later served
1 CONT the church at Germantown.  He died in 1761.  Their children were Magdalena,
1 CONT Neltgen, Hannah, Sarah, Dielman, Isaac and Maria.
1 CONT
1 CONT 4.  Johannes Kolb, born 19 May 1683; came to America with his brothers in 1707,
1 CONT and removed with them to Skippack in 1709, where on December 15th of that year
1 CONT he bought 150 acres of land.
1 CONT
1 CONT 5.  Jacob Kolb, born 21 May 1685; settled in Germantown in 1707, removed to 
1 CONT Skippack in 1709; married 1) in 1710, Sarah Van Sintern, sister to Magdalena,
1 CONT his brother Martin's wife; 2) in the old Log Mennonite Church at Germantown 
1 CONT (name unknown.)  On October 1, 1739, as he was pressing cider, the beam of the
1 CONT press fell on him crushing one side of his head and shoulder, from which injuries
1 CONT he died a half-hour later.  He was 54 years of age at the time of his death.
1 CONT He was a trustee of the Skippack Mennonite Congregation as early as 1717, and
1 CONT is, no doubt, buried in the graveyard adjoining that church.  
1 CONT
1 CONT 6.  Henry Kolb, died 1730; came to America with his brothers in 1707, and
1 CONT settled in Germantown.  He also was a minister and settled at Skippack in 1709,
1 CONT which church he served for some years.  His will is dated February 20, 1729,
1 CONT and probated the 13th of July of the following year.  His children were Peter,
1 CONT David, Dielman, Mary, Dorothy, Anne and Agnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT 7.  Dielman Kolb, Jr., born 10 Nov 1691, married 1714, Elizabeth Schnebli.
1 CONT
1 CONT ******************************
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman KOLB, youngest son of Dielman Kolb, Sr., was born 10 Nov 1691, at 1:00 
1 CONT in the afternoon, in the Palatinate (Pfalz) at Wolfsheim.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is taken from an old book which was printed in Germany in 1581,
1 CONT and was brought over to this country by Dielman Kolb and was in the possession
1 CONT of the family until 1895, when it was sold at public sale after the death of
1 CONT Daniel K. Cassel, Harleysville, PA.
1 CONT
1 CONT (Translation)  
1 CONT Anno, 1691, the 10th of November, I Dielman Kolb, was born into this
1 CONT world at one o'clock in the afternoon.
1 CONT Anno 1714, on St. Jacob's Day, in the 23rd year of my age, I was married
1 CONT to my housewife, Elizabeth.
1 CONT Anno, 1717, the 21st of March, we went to Ibersheim, in the Palatinate,
1 CONT on the journey to Pennsylvania and the 10th August, 1717, arrived safely here
1 CONT in Philadelphia.
1 CONT This book belongs to me, Dielman Kolb, and I have received it from Jacob
1 CONT Schnebli of Manheim, in the Palatinate, Anno Dom., 1722 at this time dwelling
1 CONT in Solforth township, in the County of Philadelphia, in Pennsylvania.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name of Andrew Ziegler appears as one-time owner of this book; he married
1 CONT Elizabeth, the only daughter of Dielman and Elizabeth Schnebli Kolb.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb was a Mennonite minister, living at Manheim, where he attended
1 CONT that congregation as a preacher.  He made himself both useful and most valuable
1 CONT by receiving and lodging his persecuted fellow believers who fled from Switzer-
1 CONT land, as appears from a letter dated August 27, 1710.  He in turn suffered
1 CONT persecution, which eventually led to his emigration to Pennsylvania to join his
1 CONT brothers, Martin, Jacob, Henry and John, who had preceded him ten years earlier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb purchased 225 acres of land on January 4, 1721 from Derick (Dirk
1 CONT Jansen) Johnson, weaver, and Margaretha his wife.  This tract lay in that part
1 CONT of Philadelphia County that later became Salford Township.  At a still later
1 CONT date, Salford was divided into Upper and Lower Salford, and this land fell then
1 CONT into the latter division and when Montgomery County was taken from Philadelphia,
1 CONT 1785, both the Salfords were included in the new organization.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the deed of Dirk Jansen to Dielman Kolb, the first land transaction
1 CONT which appears upon record, this tract adjoined other land of "Tilman Kolb."
1 CONT Heckler in his history of Lower Salford, says this other land was acquired by
1 CONT patent from the Commonwealth, but if so, there is no record on file at the Land
1 CONT Office in Harrisburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 2, 1721, Dielman Kolb purchased of Nicholas Scull another 107 acres of
1 CONT land adjoining his other tract bounding on the lands of Andrew Ledrach, Gerhart 
1 CONT Clemens and others.  Part of this tract is now occupied by the village of
1 CONT Lederachville.  On June 14, 1731, he added another 50 acres to his great tract.
1 CONT This last purchase he made of John Naglee.  About this time, however, he sold
1 CONT 50 acres of his land out of the northwestern corner of his farm to Gally
1 CONT Heffelfinger.  By a survey made for Andrew Ziegler, senior, his son-in-law,
1 CONT May 7, 1767, it appears that after selling these 50 acres he still had 559
1 CONT acres and eight-eight perches.  It is seen by this that Dielman Kolb was a man
1 CONT of means and owner of a considerable estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 10, 1733, Dielman Kolb sold 250 acres of his land to his stepson,
1 CONT Jacob Schnebli, or Snavely, "of the city of Manheim in the dominion or
1 CONT Principality of the Prince Palatine, on the Rhine, in High Germany," who later
1 CONT came to this country and resided on this tract until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb, junior, married in Germany, 1714, Elizabeth Schnebli, a widow,
1 CONT who had several children by her first husband, one of them being a son Isaac,
1 CONT who later settled in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, Mathias, and the above
1 CONT mentioned Jacob "Snavely." 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1728 the great road from Goshenhoppen to Skippack was laid out through
1 CONT Dielman Kolb's land, and his residence was about three-fourths of a mile
1 CONT southeast of the present village of Lederachville.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb was a minister of the Mennonite church, though, as was customary
1 CONT at that time, he engaged in other occupations as well.  Not only did he attend
1 CONT upon the Mennonite congregation at Salford, but, besides carrying on extensive
1 CONT farming, he was a weaver as well.  He appears to have been prominent in the
1 CONT affairs of the Mennonite Church, and to have been noted for his religious zeal.
1 CONT He was very intimate with Henry Funk, a minister and bishop of that faith, and
1 CONT it was through the perseverance and zeal of these two men that the Mennonite
1 CONT congregation in Salford was organized in 1738.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the fourth and fifth of October, 1738, Henry Ruth and his wife, Modlena, of
1 CONT Salford, by deed of lease and release granted to Henry Funk, Dielman Kolb,
1 CONT Christian Moyer, Jr., and Abraham Reiff ten acres and forty perches of land,
1 CONT part of his homestead tract.  Of the parties of the second part all were
1 CONT residents of Franconia Township, except Dielman Kolb, who was of Salford.  Funk
1 CONT was a bishop, Kolb a minister, and Moyer and Reiff were deacons or "Vorsteher."
1 CONT There was a building of some kind call a "tenement" on the land when it was
1 CONT purchased, but whether or not this "tenement" was turned into a meeting house
1 CONT to be used until the completion of the new one, which was immediately commenced,
1 CONT is not known, but it was not long before they were in their new church building.
1 CONT
1 CONT It appears that these four officials were considered as the trustees and that
1 CONT they, or the members of the church, were not satisfied that they should have the
1 CONT deed for the land with the meeting house thereon all in their name and power.
1 CONT Therefore, they had Robert Jones of Worcester write them a deed of conveyance,
1 CONT called a "declaration" or deed of trust, in which these four officials as 
1 CONT "parties of the first part" convey it to the church as the "second part," the
1 CONT second part being represented by seventeen members of the congregation.  This
1 CONT deed of trust was recorded in the office of the Recorder of Deeds, Norristown,
1 CONT long years after.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matters appear to have gone on very well and the church prospered.  In time, 
1 CONT however, it was considered to the advantage of their church that a new congre-
1 CONT gation be organized at Franconia, for the use and greater convenience of those
1 CONT of the faith residing in that locality, which was then called Indian Field.
1 CONT A new deed was executed, in order to convey back to the congregation in Salford
1 CONT a "Declaration of Trust" from the two surviving trustees residing in Franconia
1 CONT Township to two others of Salford.  This second paper dated March 25, 1763,
1 CONT recites that Henry Funk and Dielman Kolb are dead and Christian Moyer and
1 CONT Abraham Reiff are the survivors of the "trustees," while Jacob Clemens and
1 CONT Christian Funk represent the congregation.
1 CONT
1 CONT In connection with Bishop Henry Funk, Dielman Kolb supervised the translation
1 CONT of Jan Van Braght's "Bloedigh Tooniel" from the Dutch into the German language,
1 CONT and certified as to its correctness.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Bloedigh Tooniel", or Bloody Theatre or Martyr's Mirror, is a history of
1 CONT the "defenseless Christians, who suffered and were put to death for the testimony
1 CONT of Jesus their Saviour, from the time of Christ until the year A.D. 1660."  It
1 CONT traces the history of those Christians "who from the time of the Apostles were 
1 CONT opposed to the baptism of infants, and to warfare, including Lyonists, Petro-
1 CONT busians, and Waldenses, details the persecution of the Mennonites by the
1 CONT Spaniards in the netherlands, and the Calvinists in Switzerland, together with
1 CONT the individual sufferings of many hundred who were burned, drowned, beheaded,
1 CONT or otherwise maltreated, and contains the confession of faith adopted by the
1 CONT different communities."
1 CONT
1 CONT This book had been published in Dortrecht, July 23, 1659, the author being
1 CONT T. Jan Van Braght, and many copies were brought to this country, but they were
1 CONT all printed in the Dutch language.  It was considered necessary by the
1 CONT Mennonites that their young members should have the book to read to remind them
1 CONT of the faith and steadfastness of their fathers in the principles of the church,
1 CONT and the propriety of having it transcribed into the German script was a matter
1 CONT of consultation among prominent members of the church for some time.  The
1 CONT great difficulty was to find a translator and a printer in the wilds of
1 CONT Pennsylvania, and naturally they turned to the older and wealthier churches of
1 CONT Europe for assistance.  On October 14, 1745, Jacob Godshall of Germantown,
1 CONT Dielman Kolb of Salford, Michael Ziegler, Yellis Cassel and Martin Kolb of
1 CONT Skippack, and Heinrich Funk of Indian Creek (Franconia), sent a letter to
1 CONT Amsterdam on the subject, applying for aid.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apparently this aid did not come, but after a great deal of trouble they
1 CONT succeeded in finding both a translator and printer.  This was at Ephrata, on
1 CONT the Conestoga creek, in Lancaster County, where a little community of Dunkards
1 CONT had established themselves and introduced monastic institutions, where they
1 CONT lived single as monks, friars and nuns, holding their lands and goods in
1 CONT common.  About 1745 they secured a hand printing press and also had a paper
1 CONT mill.  Here in this quiet valley they translated and published the "Bloedigh
1 CONT Tooniel."  The first part was completed in 1748, the second part in 1749, the
1 CONT whole containing about fifteen hundred pages, and it took fifteen men three
1 CONT years to finish it.  The price per single copy was 20 shillings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb made his will July 8, 1748, and it was proved April 30, 1757, 
1 CONT nearly nine years later.  In this will, he named as trustees his "loving and
1 CONT trusty friend Henry Funk" and John Ulrich Berger.  He provided well for his
1 CONT widow, Elizabeth, during her natural life; it is not know how long she 
1 CONT survived him.   Except for 50 acres of land to be laid out of that part next
1 CONT adjoining the land he had sold to his stepson, Jacob Snebley, which he left
1 CONT to his grandson Dielman Ziegler, he bequeathed to his only child, Elizabeth,
1 CONT wife of Andrew Ziegler, Sr., all his lands, plantations and tenements whatsoever.
1 CONT To his stepson, Matthias, and granddaughter, Catherine Ziegler, he left cash
1 CONT money and to the "Congregation of ye Dutch Mennonists in Salford" he bequeathed
1 CONT 2 pounds 1 shilling and to the Mennonists of "Perkyomin & Skepack," he left a
1 CONT like sum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb died December 28, 1756.  David Shultze, the surveyor, entered upon
1 CONT his notebook the following under date of December 28, 1756:  "Der alte Dielman
1 CONT Kolb zu Shippack is auch gestorben d. 28th xbr."  And then on December 30:
1 CONT "Went to Shippack to the burial."  While there is no doubt that he was buried
1 CONT in the graveyard adjoining the church in Lower Salford, which he so long and
1 CONT faithfully served, there is no stone standing today to mark his last resting
1 CONT place.  He was in his sixty-seventh year, not quite having reached the alotted
1 CONT three score and ten.  "Few men, even in that day of heroic characters, can lay
1 CONT claim to a nobler life, and while no monument marks his last resting place, his
1 CONT name for all time will be indissolubly linked with that great historical work
1 CONT of the Pennsylvania Mennonites, the most durable monument of the denomination.
1 CONT
1 CONT Child of Dielman Kolb and Elizabeth Schnebli:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.  Elizabeth Kolb, born about 1715; married Andrew Ziegler, Sr.; resided on
1 CONT the farm near Lederachville.
0 @N6000000000587297232@ NOTE
1 CONT 6.  Henry Kolb, died 1730; came to America with his brothers in 1707, and
1 CONT settled in Germantown.  He also was a minister and settled at Skippack in 1709,
1 CONT which church he served for some years.  His will is dated February 20, 1729,
1 CONT and probated the 13th of July of the following year.  His children were Peter,
1 CONT David, Dielman, Mary, Dorothy, Anne and Agnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT (See entire note under Deilman Kolb Sr.)
0 @N6000000000587297200@ NOTE
1 CONT 4.  Johannes Kolb, born 19 May 1683; came to America with his brothers in 1707,
1 CONT and removed with them to Skippack in 1709, where on December 15th of that year
1 CONT he bought 150 acres of land.
1 CONT
1 CONT (See entire note under Deilman Kolb Sr.)
0 @N6000000000587297191@ NOTE
1 CONT 3.  Martin Kolb, born 1680; came to Pennsylvana and settled in Germantown in
1 CONT 1707; married Mary Magdalena, daughter of Isaac Van Sintern and his wife Neeltje
1 CONT Claasen.  They moved to Skippack, then Philadelphia (now Montgomery) County, in
1 CONT 1709, where he was ordained a minister of the Mennonite faith, and later served
1 CONT the church at Germantown.  He died in 1761.  Their children were Magdalena,
1 CONT Neltgen, Hannah, Sarah, Dielman, Isaac and Maria.
1 CONT
1 CONT (See entire note under Deilman Kolb Sr.)
0 @N6000000000587297247@ NOTE
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman KOLB, youngest son of Dielman Kolb, Sr., was born 10 Nov 1691, at 1:00 
1 CONT in the afternoon, in the Palatinate (Pfalz) at Wolfsheim.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is taken from an old book which was printed in Germany in 1581,
1 CONT and was brought over to this country by Dielman Kolb and was in the possession
1 CONT of the family until 1895, when it was sold at public sale after the death of
1 CONT Daniel K. Cassel, Harleysville, PA.
1 CONT
1 CONT (Translation)  
1 CONT Anno, 1691, the 10th of November, I Dielman Kolb, was born into this
1 CONT world at one o'clock in the afternoon.
1 CONT Anno 1714, on St. Jacob's Day, in the 23rd year of my age, I was married
1 CONT to my housewife, Elizabeth.
1 CONT Anno, 1717, the 21st of March, we went to Ibersheim, in the Palatinate,
1 CONT on the journey to Pennsylvania and the 10th August, 1717, arrived safely here
1 CONT in Philadelphia.
1 CONT This book belongs to me, Dielman Kolb, and I have received it from Jacob
1 CONT Schnebli of Manheim, in the Palatinate, Anno Dom., 1722 at this time dwelling
1 CONT in Solforth township, in the County of Philadelphia, in Pennsylvania.
1 CONT
1 CONT The name of Andrew Ziegler appears as one-time owner of this book; he married
1 CONT Elizabeth, the only daughter of Dielman and Elizabeth Schnebli Kolb.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb was a Mennonite minister, living at Manheim, where he attended
1 CONT that congregation as a preacher.  He made himself both useful and most valuable
1 CONT by receiving and lodging his persecuted fellow believers who fled from Switzer-
1 CONT land, as appears from a letter dated August 27, 1710.  He in turn suffered
1 CONT persecution, which eventually led to his emigration to Pennsylvania to join his
1 CONT brothers, Martin, Jacob, Henry and John, who had preceded him ten years earlier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb purchased 225 acres of land on January 4, 1721 from Derick (Dirk
1 CONT Jansen) Johnson, weaver, and Margaretha his wife.  This tract lay in that part
1 CONT of Philadelphia County that later became Salford Township.  At a still later
1 CONT date, Salford was divided into Upper and Lower Salford, and this land fell then
1 CONT into the latter division and when Montgomery County was taken from Philadelphia,
1 CONT 1785, both the Salfords were included in the new organization.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the deed of Dirk Jansen to Dielman Kolb, the first land transaction
1 CONT which appears upon record, this tract adjoined other land of "Tilman Kolb."
1 CONT Heckler in his history of Lower Salford, says this other land was acquired by
1 CONT patent from the Commonwealth, but if so, there is no record on file at the Land
1 CONT Office in Harrisburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT On April 2, 1721, Dielman Kolb purchased of Nicholas Scull another 107 acres of
1 CONT land adjoining his other tract bounding on the lands of Andrew Ledrach, Gerhart 
1 CONT Clemens and others.  Part of this tract is now occupied by the village of
1 CONT Lederachville.  On June 14, 1731, he added another 50 acres to his great tract.
1 CONT This last purchase he made of John Naglee.  About this time, however, he sold
1 CONT 50 acres of his land out of the northwestern corner of his farm to Gally
1 CONT Heffelfinger.  By a survey made for Andrew Ziegler, senior, his son-in-law,
1 CONT May 7, 1767, it appears that after selling these 50 acres he still had 559
1 CONT acres and eight-eight perches.  It is seen by this that Dielman Kolb was a man
1 CONT of means and owner of a considerable estate.
1 CONT
1 CONT On October 10, 1733, Dielman Kolb sold 250 acres of his land to his stepson,
1 CONT Jacob Schnebli, or Snavely, "of the city of Manheim in the dominion or
1 CONT Principality of the Prince Palatine, on the Rhine, in High Germany," who later
1 CONT came to this country and resided on this tract until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb, junior, married in Germany, 1714, Elizabeth Schnebli, a widow,
1 CONT who had several children by her first husband, one of them being a son Isaac,
1 CONT who later settled in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, Mathias, and the above
1 CONT mentioned Jacob "Snavely." 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1728 the great road from Goshenhoppen to Skippack was laid out through
1 CONT Dielman Kolb's land, and his residence was about three-fourths of a mile
1 CONT southeast of the present village of Lederachville.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb was a minister of the Mennonite church, though, as was customary
1 CONT at that time, he engaged in other occupations as well.  Not only did he attend
1 CONT upon the Mennonite congregation at Salford, but, besides carrying on extensive
1 CONT farming, he was a weaver as well.  He appears to have been prominent in the
1 CONT affairs of the Mennonite Church, and to have been noted for his religious zeal.
1 CONT He was very intimate with Henry Funk, a minister and bishop of that faith, and
1 CONT it was through the perseverance and zeal of these two men that the Mennonite
1 CONT congregation in Salford was organized in 1738.
1 CONT
1 CONT On the fourth and fifth of October, 1738, Henry Ruth and his wife, Modlena, of
1 CONT Salford, by deed of lease and release granted to Henry Funk, Dielman Kolb,
1 CONT Christian Moyer, Jr., and Abraham Reiff ten acres and forty perches of land,
1 CONT part of his homestead tract.  Of the parties of the second part all were
1 CONT residents of Franconia Township, except Dielman Kolb, who was of Salford.  Funk
1 CONT was a bishop, Kolb a minister, and Moyer and Reiff were deacons or "Vorsteher."
1 CONT There was a building of some kind call a "tenement" on the land when it was
1 CONT purchased, but whether or not this "tenement" was turned into a meeting house
1 CONT to be used until the completion of the new one, which was immediately commenced,
1 CONT is not known, but it was not long before they were in their new church building.
1 CONT
1 CONT It appears that these four officials were considered as the trustees and that
1 CONT they, or the members of the church, were not satisfied that they should have the
1 CONT deed for the land with the meeting house thereon all in their name and power.
1 CONT Therefore, they had Robert Jones of Worcester write them a deed of conveyance,
1 CONT called a "declaration" or deed of trust, in which these four officials as 
1 CONT "parties of the first part" convey it to the church as the "second part," the
1 CONT second part being represented by seventeen members of the congregation.  This
1 CONT deed of trust was recorded in the office of the Recorder of Deeds, Norristown,
1 CONT long years after.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matters appear to have gone on very well and the church prospered.  In time, 
1 CONT however, it was considered to the advantage of their church that a new congre-
1 CONT gation be organized at Franconia, for the use and greater convenience of those
1 CONT of the faith residing in that locality, which was then called Indian Field.
1 CONT A new deed was executed, in order to convey back to the congregation in Salford
1 CONT a "Declaration of Trust" from the two surviving trustees residing in Franconia
1 CONT Township to two others of Salford.  This second paper dated March 25, 1763,
1 CONT recites that Henry Funk and Dielman Kolb are dead and Christian Moyer and
1 CONT Abraham Reiff are the survivors of the "trustees," while Jacob Clemens and
1 CONT Christian Funk represent the congregation.
1 CONT
1 CONT In connection with Bishop Henry Funk, Dielman Kolb supervised the translation
1 CONT of Jan Van Braght's "Bloedigh Tooniel" from the Dutch into the German language,
1 CONT and certified as to its correctness.
1 CONT
1 CONT "The Bloedigh Tooniel", or Bloody Theatre or Martyr's Mirror, is a history of
1 CONT the "defenseless Christians, who suffered and were put to death for the testimony
1 CONT of Jesus their Saviour, from the time of Christ until the year A.D. 1660."  It
1 CONT traces the history of those Christians "who from the time of the Apostles were 
1 CONT opposed to the baptism of infants, and to warfare, including Lyonists, Petro-
1 CONT busians, and Waldenses, details the persecution of the Mennonites by the
1 CONT Spaniards in the netherlands, and the Calvinists in Switzerland, together with
1 CONT the individual sufferings of many hundred who were burned, drowned, beheaded,
1 CONT or otherwise maltreated, and contains the confession of faith adopted by the
1 CONT different communities."
1 CONT
1 CONT This book had been published in Dortrecht, July 23, 1659, the author being
1 CONT T. Jan Van Braght, and many copies were brought to this country, but they were
1 CONT all printed in the Dutch language.  It was considered necessary by the
1 CONT Mennonites that their young members should have the book to read to remind them
1 CONT of the faith and steadfastness of their fathers in the principles of the church,
1 CONT and the propriety of having it transcribed into the German script was a matter
1 CONT of consultation among prominent members of the church for some time.  The
1 CONT great difficulty was to find a translator and a printer in the wilds of
1 CONT Pennsylvania, and naturally they turned to the older and wealthier churches of
1 CONT Europe for assistance.  On October 14, 1745, Jacob Godshall of Germantown,
1 CONT Dielman Kolb of Salford, Michael Ziegler, Yellis Cassel and Martin Kolb of
1 CONT Skippack, and Heinrich Funk of Indian Creek (Franconia), sent a letter to
1 CONT Amsterdam on the subject, applying for aid.
1 CONT
1 CONT Apparently this aid did not come, but after a great deal of trouble they
1 CONT succeeded in finding both a translator and printer.  This was at Ephrata, on
1 CONT the Conestoga creek, in Lancaster County, where a little community of Dunkards
1 CONT had established themselves and introduced monastic institutions, where they
1 CONT lived single as monks, friars and nuns, holding their lands and goods in
1 CONT common.  About 1745 they secured a hand printing press and also had a paper
1 CONT mill.  Here in this quiet valley they translated and published the "Bloedigh
1 CONT Tooniel."  The first part was completed in 1748, the second part in 1749, the
1 CONT whole containing about fifteen hundred pages, and it took fifteen men three
1 CONT years to finish it.  The price per single copy was 20 shillings.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb made his will July 8, 1748, and it was proved April 30, 1757, 
1 CONT nearly nine years later.  In this will, he named as trustees his "loving and
1 CONT trusty friend Henry Funk" and John Ulrich Berger.  He provided well for his
1 CONT widow, Elizabeth, during her natural life; it is not know how long she 
1 CONT survived him.   Except for 50 acres of land to be laid out of that part next
1 CONT adjoining the land he had sold to his stepson, Jacob Snebley, which he left
1 CONT to his grandson Dielman Ziegler, he bequeathed to his only child, Elizabeth,
1 CONT wife of Andrew Ziegler, Sr., all his lands, plantations and tenements whatsoever.
1 CONT To his stepson, Matthias, and granddaughter, Catherine Ziegler, he left cash
1 CONT money and to the "Congregation of ye Dutch Mennonists in Salford" he bequeathed
1 CONT 2 pounds 1 shilling and to the Mennonists of "Perkyomin & Skepack," he left a
1 CONT like sum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dielman Kolb died December 28, 1756.  David Shultze, the surveyor, entered upon
1 CONT his notebook the following under date of December 28, 1756:  "Der alte Dielman
1 CONT Kolb zu Shippack is auch gestorben d. 28th xbr."  And then on December 30:
1 CONT "Went to Shippack to the burial."  While there is no doubt that he was buried
1 CONT in the graveyard adjoining the church in Lower Salford, which he so long and
1 CONT faithfully served, there is no stone standing today to mark his last resting
