1 CONT Came to America in 1732
1 CONT
1 CONT Elmwood, Ont., Canada
1 CONT May 16, 1893
1 CONT Dear Brother Jacob,
1 CONT I should have told you before that I returned from my Pennsylvania trip.  I enjoyed myself very much.  I found three first cousins, Joseph Dirstine, age 77, Henry Dirstine, age 79 and Mrs. Sadding, maiden name Moyer, age 80.  I ha
1 CONC ppened to strike the old Pioneer homestead of Michael Dirstine, who was born in 1712 and left Germany for PA in 1732. He built a sawmill and grist
1 CONT mill in 1742, which his descendants of the sixth generation are yet operating.  I was in the old house built in 1848, a substantial structure 28 x 43 and two stories high and good for another hundred years, though unoccupied, the 
1 CONC present owners having built a new house. 
1 CONT I went to the old cemetery three different times before I found engraved on an old field stone, "Michael Dirstine Died June 6, 1777 aged 64 years, 11 months, 6 days."  He is the ancestor of all the Dirstines born in America.
1 CONT He took up a considerable tract of land close by Sellerstown, Bucks County about 1732, on which I find some five or six members of his descendants yet living and well to do.  Their great Grandfather and our great grandfather were 
1 CONC brothers.  We agreed to erect to our worthy ancestor a new gravestone with as much history as we could conveniently put on.  Will you let me know the reason why you and Henry wrote "Dirstein"? I have been asked why we do not all w
1 CONC rite our names alike.
1 CONT We are all well, thank God, and trust you may all enjoy the same blessing
1 CONT Your affectionate brother, John Dirstine
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Michael Dirstine came to America in 1732 at the age of 20 in the ship "Samuel>" from Rotterdam, landing at Philadelphia in a company listed as Palatines.  He was doubtless also from Palatinate (a district of Germany, a part of Bav
1 CONC aria, Speyer is the seat of Government).
1 CONT He brought with him a large bible which, tradition says, contained records of the family for about two hundred years before his departure from Germany.  Family tradition also connects this family with "Castle Durnstein" on the Dan
1 CONC ube (See illustration in National Geographic for Dec. 1929, page 689, with Queen Ann of Denmark.  As Elizabeth, daughter of James I of England and Ann of Denmark, married Frederick V, Elector of the Palatinate and King of Bohemia,
1 CONC  the origin of Durnstein Castle goes back to the 12th century.)
1 CONT This old bible handed down in the family in line of the youngest sons was in 1897 in the possession of John Dirstine of Elmwood, Ont.  Many years previously it had been through a fire, and the records back of the birth of Michael 
1 CONC and his brothers and sisters has been obliterated.  The later
1 CONT entries, however, gave an authentic basis for the study of the Dirstine family carried on so diligently by the late Dr. Frank H. Dirstine of Cleveland, Ohio.
1 CONT On Jan. 18, 1736 Michael Dirstine purchased from his wife's father a farm of 217 1/2 acres in Rockhill Township, Bucks County, PA, located 1 1/2 miles south of Sellersville, on a stream, upon the banks of which he built a sawmill 
1 CONC and later a grist mill, which property has remained in the possession of his descendants down to David Dirstine with whom Dr. Dirstine corresponded about 1901.  For this property he paid 100 pounds in "lawful money of the Province
1 CONC ."
1 CONT Being a Mennonite German Quaker, his name appears in a list of those who subscribed to certain qualifications prepared for them, to replace the usual oath as follows: "The following persons being Quakers or such who conscientiousl
1 CONC y scruple to take an oath, being also foreigners and having
1 CONT complied with the terms required by the Act of Parliament, etc.--"
1 CONT
1 CONT Our father's lamp and mother's light : ten generations of the Dierstein family in North America 
1 CONT Authors:    Harris, Joy Derstine , 1936- (Main Author) 
1 CONT "List of sources:" p. 266-267. Includes index (listing Derstein, Derstine, Dirstein, Dirstine, Durstine and several  other variant spellings). Includes Alderfer, Detweiler, Freed, Godshall, Landis (Landes), Moyer and related famil
1 CONC ies.
1 CONT Michael Dierstein (1712-1777), a Mennonite and the son of Samuel Dierstein, immigrated from the Palatinate of Germany to Rockhill Township, Bucks County,  Pennsylvania, and married Agnes Kolb in 1734. Descendants and relatives liv
1 CONC ed in  Pennsylvania, Ohio, Michigan, Minnesota, California and elsewhere. Some  descendants immigrated to Ontario and elsewhere in Canada. 
1 CONT
1 CONT THE HISTORY OF BUCKS COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA, CHAPTER XXXIII, ROCKHILL, 1740 from the discovery of the Delaware to the present time by W. W. H. Davis, A.M., 1876 and 1905* editions..
1 CONT
1 CONT Derstein's mill, 1/1/2 miles south of Sellersville, on the North Pennsylvania railroad, is one of the oldest mills in the upper end of the county. It is thought to have been the first one erected between Whitemarsh and Centre Vall
1 CONC ey. The first mill was built by the ancestor of the Derstein family prior to 1742, and in the rudest manner. Tradition says that four saplings were planted in the ground and covered with a straw-roof; the mill works constructed un
1 CONC derneath were of the simplest description, but sufficient to turn a pair of chopping stones. A second mill was erected by Abraham and Michael Derstein, 1742, with all the improvements known at that day. A culvert built across the 
1 CONC road over the tail race remains to this day, and is as sound as when the masons finished 
1 CONT the work. In 1873 William and David Derstein erected a third mill on the site of the old one, which is complete in all its appointments. 
1 CONT
