1 CONT Abraham inherited the land in Hilltown Twp. from his father and farmed there “near Albright’s Corner,” later known as “Loux Corner.”  It is believed they were Mennonites, although no records indicate that, and place of burial has 
1 CONC not been located.  the Blooming Glen Mennonite Church would be nearest to that area, and there is an Esther Dirstine buried there, as mentioned above, but as she was the third wife, she may have been buried where her parents atten
1 CONC ded, and not with her husband.
1 CONT Abraham was hit harder with Militia fines for non-attendance upon exercise than his brother, Isaac.  Being in Hilltown Twp., he was under Capt. Grier’s Company.
1 CONT April May 1780, Abraham Duston, 72-0-0;  Aug. 1780 None; Fall 1780 Abraham Dustin,    90-9-0;  Spring 1781, Abraham Destin, 1-4-0;  Sept. 1781, Abraham Denton, 13-5-0; Fall 1782, Abraham Duston, 1-8-0
1 CONT During the War of Revolution or the War of 1812, soldiers camped on Abraham’s farm and he hid the horses in the swamp and saved his grain by putting chaff over the grain.  p. 46
0 @N6000000000587296399@ NOTE
1 CONT The family believed to have lived in Cleveland, Ohio
0 @N6000000004556519917@ NOTE [ishmaelz.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was the son of Garret Hunsicker and grandson of Rev. Henry Hu nsicker. He enjoyed only such advantages as were to be found at the sch ools adjacent to his home, after which he engaged in active labor. He w as identified with
1 CONC  the business interests of the county, having served f or 20 years as director of the Montgomery National Bank and 10 years di rector of the Montgomery Mutual Fire and Storm Insurance Company.  He w as successful in business and b
1 CONC ecame wealthy.
0 @N6000000000141950240@ NOTE Anna Catharine Reutlinger born August 3, 1869.  Unmarried but a faithful servant of the Lord who had a great part in my (Bill Silbert's) development as a Christian.  She was Superintendent of the Junior Christian Endeavor Society 
1 CONC at our church (Mt. Airy Presbytarian); also served on the board of managers of the Presbytarian Home for Widows and Single Women in West Philadelphia and did prectical nursing.  She lived with and cared for her mother and father a
1 CONC t 42 Meehan Avenue, Mt. Airy, and then after they had died, lived there until it was necessary to sell her home when she had to be put in a Nursing facility.
1 CONT -from Bill Silbert information
0 @N6000000000141947385@ NOTE Emma was a teacher in Philadelphia, married Ned Whittemore & moved to Nashua.  She was a natural mimic.       
0 @N6000000004272395352@ NOTE one of his daughters is married to a male Knechtel, see http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/e/a/April-Virginia-Pearson/PDFGENEO1.pdf
